1
00:01:33,527 --> 00:01:36,508
This is it, Rupert.
Our big chance.

2
00:01:36,997 --> 00:01:39,477
Maybe next week, your
name will go up in lights.

3
00:01:39,533 --> 00:01:42,013
Rupert, the talk of the town.

4
00:01:42,169 --> 00:01:44,740
You won't let me
down, will you, Rupert?

5
00:01:44,806 --> 00:01:47,192
You'll do everything
just like I taught you?

6
00:01:47,306 --> 00:01:49,152
Ah yes, now...

7
00:01:49,377 --> 00:01:52,881
Now, how about a
dress rehearsal? Here.

8
00:01:54,415 --> 00:01:57,021
<i>The curtain is up.
You're on the stage.</i>

9
00:01:57,685 --> 00:02:00,462
<i>Stay right there
when I tell you.</i>

10
00:02:00,556 --> 00:02:03,400
Now get ready for
our song and dance.

11
00:02:03,459 --> 00:02:04,870
Are you all set?

12
00:02:05,527 --> 00:02:07,370
One, two...

13
00:02:54,613 --> 00:02:57,150
Now we've got to do
the dance in unison.

14
00:02:57,215 --> 00:03:01,664
And it's got to be perfect, for
everything depends on how we do it.

15
00:03:03,356 --> 00:03:05,893
<i>You all set? Here we go.</i>

16
00:03:27,481 --> 00:03:30,587
- Phil, I'm so glad to see you.
- Joe, how have you been?

17
00:03:30,651 --> 00:03:33,655
Not too good, but
everything's gonna change now.

18
00:03:33,787 --> 00:03:35,790
Sit down. How are you, miss?

19
00:03:35,857 --> 00:03:38,895
This is Joe Mahoney. He used
to work out of my father's office.

20
00:03:38,960 --> 00:03:42,373
And the best lion-tamer in
the business. Weren't you?

21
00:03:42,430 --> 00:03:44,467
- Go on with you.
- Delighted, I'm sure.

22
00:03:44,532 --> 00:03:45,772
Thank you.

23
00:03:45,834 --> 00:03:48,007
What's this new act
that you wired me about?

24
00:03:48,069 --> 00:03:50,710
Here it is. Now greet
the man, Rupert.

25
00:03:56,645 --> 00:03:58,886
You mean, that's it?

26
00:03:58,981 --> 00:04:00,289
That's it.

27
00:04:00,483 --> 00:04:02,156
- A squirrel?
- Sure.

28
00:04:02,384 --> 00:04:06,162
- You wouldn't fool me, would you?
- He's a great performer.

29
00:04:06,356 --> 00:04:09,565
Something like Rupert only
happens once in a million years.

30
00:04:09,626 --> 00:04:13,540
I'm sorry, Joe, but
I'm not interested.

31
00:04:13,730 --> 00:04:16,711
- He's not box office.
- Come on, honey, we're late.

32
00:04:18,368 --> 00:04:22,078
- Phil, you haven't seen what he can do.
- I've seen enough already.

33
00:04:22,173 --> 00:04:23,208
Joe, look...

34
00:04:23,274 --> 00:04:25,720
a lion is a big, savage beast.

35
00:04:25,910 --> 00:04:27,787
He's vicious, ruthless.

36
00:04:27,845 --> 00:04:31,292
A bloodthirsty killer from the
faraway jungles of darkest Africa.

37
00:04:31,349 --> 00:04:32,760
So he's box office.

38
00:04:32,817 --> 00:04:35,764
<i>But a little pipsqueak
thing like a squirrel...</i>

39
00:04:35,820 --> 00:04:39,701
<i>that anybody can see
anyplace, in any park, anytime...</i>

40
00:04:40,159 --> 00:04:43,003
Besides, he's so small, they'd
never see him in the balcony.

41
00:04:43,061 --> 00:04:45,769
But I tell you,
Rupert's almost human.

42
00:04:45,864 --> 00:04:48,174
- Come on, honey, we're late.
- Just a minute.

43
00:04:48,233 --> 00:04:49,473
Look, Joe...

44
00:04:49,535 --> 00:04:52,813
I'd love to give you a break. I
know things have been tough.

45
00:04:54,274 --> 00:04:57,050
- But he just ain't...
- Box office.

46
00:05:19,300 --> 00:05:23,113
Goodbye, Mr. Dingle. I'll pay
you every cent that I owe you.

47
00:05:25,740 --> 00:05:27,777
You should, Mahoney.

48
00:05:35,650 --> 00:05:39,497
Pete. Get a broom and pail,
and help me clean this place out.

49
00:06:01,745 --> 00:06:06,251
It's nice and peaceful here,
ain't it? You can breathe.

50
00:06:07,451 --> 00:06:11,559
Take a good look around
at some of your new pals.

51
00:06:12,690 --> 00:06:15,899
I want you to go on and get
acquainted with them. Come on.

52
00:06:16,761 --> 00:06:18,399
<i>That's it.</i>

53
00:06:19,731 --> 00:06:23,577
Don't be stubborn
about this. Go on.

54
00:06:27,106 --> 00:06:30,918
<i>To be sure, it's a silly outfit.</i>

55
00:06:32,411 --> 00:06:35,620
There you are. They
would have laughed at you.

56
00:06:38,550 --> 00:06:41,828
Now, go on. Yes, I
know you don't want to.

57
00:06:42,756 --> 00:06:44,895
<i>I know you'll be happy here.</i>

58
00:06:45,492 --> 00:06:48,439
I know they'll love you.
You'll be the life of the party...

59
00:06:48,495 --> 00:06:52,568
doing the somersaults and all
the cute little tricks that I taught you.

60
00:06:53,166 --> 00:06:55,043
<i>Go on, I tell you.</i>

61
00:06:56,069 --> 00:06:57,674
<i>Beat it, I tell you.</i>

62
00:06:58,439 --> 00:07:00,112
Beat it, I tell you.

63
00:07:05,146 --> 00:07:06,955
What shall I do?

64
00:07:09,684 --> 00:07:11,789
You just ain't box office.

65
00:07:12,721 --> 00:07:16,498
Papa, do you think we might have
some money in that Chicago account?{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chicago}

66
00:07:16,558 --> 00:07:20,506
I heard from that bank last week.
I think I got the letter with me.

67
00:07:23,966 --> 00:07:25,502
Here it is.

68
00:07:27,870 --> 00:07:32,751
It says, "Dear Mr. Amendola, this is the
last monthly statement we will mail you.

69
00:07:33,009 --> 00:07:37,685
"We feel it is hardly worth three
cents to tell you you have two cents."

70
00:07:38,815 --> 00:07:40,089
Let's get going.

71
00:07:40,149 --> 00:07:44,223
Look at all those lucky people,
hurrying home with arms full of packages.

72
00:07:44,321 --> 00:07:47,894
They'll have turkey for
Christmas, packages under the tree.

73
00:07:47,992 --> 00:07:50,063
Don't envy them, Rosalinda.

74
00:07:50,127 --> 00:07:53,836
I bet every one of them faced a
Christmas like us at some time or another.

75
00:07:53,897 --> 00:07:58,778
Besides, it ain't Christmas yet. Saint
Nick may have a bundle for us, too.

76
00:07:59,137 --> 00:08:02,482
But the trouble is, we ain't got
no address he can deliver them to.

77
00:08:02,540 --> 00:08:05,384
Amendola! The Amendola Trio!

78
00:08:05,443 --> 00:08:08,947
- Joe Mahoney!
- Joe, darling!

79
00:08:09,014 --> 00:08:11,290
Now, don't tell me
this is little Rosie.

80
00:08:11,349 --> 00:08:14,888
She's certainly growing
fast. A little too fast.

81
00:08:14,954 --> 00:08:17,594
It seems like only yesterday
I bought her them shoes...

82
00:08:17,656 --> 00:08:20,227
and already she's
complaining they're too small.

83
00:08:20,292 --> 00:08:22,772
And with you? How's
the act been going?

84
00:08:22,995 --> 00:08:25,407
Us? Great. Sensational.

85
00:08:25,498 --> 00:08:28,911
If I made any more money,
I'd have to buy myself a wallet.

86
00:08:29,135 --> 00:08:31,013
And you know something?

87
00:08:31,104 --> 00:08:34,779
We've played the circuit so
many times, just for a change...

88
00:08:34,841 --> 00:08:36,343
we booked a European tour.

89
00:08:36,410 --> 00:08:39,254
- Europe?
- London, Paris, Budapest.{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/London}

90
00:08:39,613 --> 00:08:43,356
And in India, we gave a performance
the audience will never forget.

91
00:08:43,450 --> 00:08:46,398
- Why?
- Because they were elephants.

92
00:08:48,956 --> 00:08:52,028
Please, a little respect for
that joke. It's older than you.

93
00:08:53,494 --> 00:08:54,939
<i>Sorry, Joe.</i>

94
00:08:55,830 --> 00:08:58,504
I see you haven't
been doing so good.

95
00:08:59,367 --> 00:09:03,373
- To tell you the truth, it has been a little...
- You don't have to tell me.

96
00:09:03,439 --> 00:09:07,979
It's the same with us. We haven't
worked since Rosie cut her second teeth.

97
00:09:09,278 --> 00:09:13,021
- Joe, do you live around here?
- I do. I did.

98
00:09:13,082 --> 00:09:15,995
<i>I just moved out less
than a half-hour ago.</i>

99
00:09:16,151 --> 00:09:19,497
- Is the place rented yet?
- I don't think so.

100
00:09:19,556 --> 00:09:21,900
- How much rent did you pay?
- I didn't.

101
00:09:21,958 --> 00:09:25,269
But if you've got $32,
you can move right in.

102
00:09:26,996 --> 00:09:29,374
How long did you live there
without paying any rent?

103
00:09:29,432 --> 00:09:30,604
Six months.

104
00:09:30,667 --> 00:09:34,047
Just what we're looking for.
Something out of the high-rent district.

105
00:09:34,238 --> 00:09:38,015
- It's been good seeing you.
- You must drop in and see us soon.

106
00:09:38,075 --> 00:09:39,520
Yes, thank you.

107
00:09:40,578 --> 00:09:42,387
Wait for me!

108
00:09:43,247 --> 00:09:46,854
- Hadn't you better ask him where it is?
- We ain't got no time.

109
00:09:48,420 --> 00:09:49,421
Joe!

110
00:09:51,823 --> 00:09:56,135
- Where is it?
- 322-and-a-half Maple Street. Over there.

111
00:09:56,394 --> 00:09:58,806
Come on back. It's this way.

112
00:10:03,135 --> 00:10:04,843
Wait for me!

113
00:10:05,905 --> 00:10:07,612
Merry Christmas.

114
00:10:39,207 --> 00:10:40,777
Come here. Here, boy.

115
00:11:35,066 --> 00:11:36,067
Hello.

116
00:11:36,133 --> 00:11:38,705
Pardon this
unseemly protrusion...

117
00:11:38,770 --> 00:11:41,683
but after reading this sign, I
took the liberty of removing it.

118
00:11:41,740 --> 00:11:45,415
- I don't think you'll need it anymore.
- You wanna rent this place?

119
00:11:45,911 --> 00:11:49,654
We might work something out,
if you don't drag lawyers into this.

120
00:11:49,815 --> 00:11:53,525
You'd better speak to my father
about it. He's right next door.

121
00:11:55,688 --> 00:11:59,033
On second thought, I
can handle this myself.

122
00:12:00,927 --> 00:12:02,133
Come in.

123
00:12:02,462 --> 00:12:04,499
Be back in a minute.

124
00:12:21,148 --> 00:12:23,389
Gosh, it's good to be home.

125
00:12:23,484 --> 00:12:26,796
- My name is Amendola.
- My name is Pete Dingle.

126
00:12:28,857 --> 00:12:30,234
Rosalinda.

127
00:12:30,558 --> 00:12:32,299
Joe Mahoney sent us.

128
00:12:33,428 --> 00:12:37,137
That's okay with me, but
don't mention it to my father.

129
00:12:37,766 --> 00:12:39,974
The rent will be $32 a month.

130
00:12:40,836 --> 00:12:44,010
I don't mind electric
bulbs that are unfrosted.

131
00:12:45,675 --> 00:12:49,748
I don't mind beds with
springs that fight back...

132
00:12:51,347 --> 00:12:54,419
or window shades that
curl up like anchovies.

133
00:12:54,884 --> 00:12:59,129
But there's one thing I
can't stand. Leaky faucets.

134
00:13:02,793 --> 00:13:04,864
- Do you mind?
- Not at all.

135
00:13:08,098 --> 00:13:09,202
Perfect.

136
00:13:09,466 --> 00:13:12,676
Mama, you might as
well start unpacking.

137
00:13:12,770 --> 00:13:16,081
This ain't exactly the
bridal suite at the Waldorf.

138
00:13:16,140 --> 00:13:18,916
But who knows? If we give
this place a good cleaning...

139
00:13:18,976 --> 00:13:22,856
- we might find an extra room.
- "Good cleaning" is right.

140
00:13:23,214 --> 00:13:25,057
Look at all those cobwebs.

141
00:13:25,116 --> 00:13:29,122
Don't touch those cobwebs. They're
probably holding the building together.

142
00:13:29,655 --> 00:13:31,931
Now that all the business
has been taken care of...

143
00:13:31,990 --> 00:13:35,699
But you see, my father, unfortunately,
wants his money in advance.

144
00:13:35,761 --> 00:13:40,733
And a lucky man he is, too, your father,
having a son so gifted in the art of music.

145
00:13:41,200 --> 00:13:42,976
I just fool around...

146
00:13:43,036 --> 00:13:46,176
I know. I, too, am a musician.

147
00:13:46,406 --> 00:13:49,751
Modesty compels me to
admit that I play a pretty piano...

148
00:13:49,809 --> 00:13:53,222
and if necessary, I can
even play an ugly one.

149
00:13:54,614 --> 00:13:57,254
Don't forget. Feel free
to drop in at any time.

150
00:13:57,785 --> 00:13:58,991
Thanks very much.

151
00:13:59,286 --> 00:14:00,458
About the rent...

152
00:14:00,521 --> 00:14:03,593
- I think I'm gonna like it here.
- I hope so.

153
00:14:04,191 --> 00:14:07,138
Now, if you don't
mind, I'd like to change.

154
00:14:07,194 --> 00:14:09,572
No, not at all. I mean...

155
00:14:10,197 --> 00:14:12,234
I'll see you later.

156
00:14:19,341 --> 00:14:23,619
- Well, Mama, I did it.
- Look, Pa, he forgot his tuba.

157
00:14:23,678 --> 00:14:28,457
He'll be back. But if he don't, you can
blow on it every night and announce dinner.

158
00:14:30,019 --> 00:14:31,862
<i>You realize what I've done?</i>

159
00:14:31,954 --> 00:14:34,867
<i>No rent in advance, and
a roof over our heads.</i>

160
00:14:37,326 --> 00:14:39,704
There's an awful big
hole in the skylight.

161
00:14:41,197 --> 00:14:45,806
So what? If it snows, we'll be the only
ones in town to have a white Christmas...

162
00:14:45,869 --> 00:14:48,475
inside the house and out.

163
00:14:49,273 --> 00:14:50,980
- Hello, Pete.
- Hi.

164
00:14:51,041 --> 00:14:53,282
I think I got a letter for you.

165
00:14:53,410 --> 00:14:56,084
- No, it's for your old man.
- I'll give it to him.

166
00:14:56,146 --> 00:14:58,717
If I don't see you
tomorrow, merry Christmas.

167
00:14:59,884 --> 00:15:01,295
Same to you.

168
00:15:07,859 --> 00:15:11,068
- Did you clean the place?
- Clean it? I rented it.

169
00:15:11,129 --> 00:15:13,666
Good, you can work if
you make up your mind.

170
00:15:13,765 --> 00:15:16,474
- Who'd you rent it to?
- Their name is Amendola.

171
00:15:16,802 --> 00:15:17,803
Their?

172
00:15:18,404 --> 00:15:20,680
- How many are there?
- Just three.

173
00:15:20,739 --> 00:15:22,047
They're awfully nice people.

174
00:15:22,107 --> 00:15:25,020
That's good for a
change. I'll take the money.

175
00:15:26,545 --> 00:15:28,491
There's a mother and a father...

176
00:15:28,547 --> 00:15:31,392
- and a daughter.
- Okay, give me the $32.

177
00:15:32,219 --> 00:15:36,463
They didn't even ask for any
redecorations or repairing anything.

178
00:15:36,656 --> 00:15:38,658
That saves us a
lot of money, Pa.

179
00:15:38,725 --> 00:15:41,365
Don't tell me you let
them stay without paying.

180
00:15:41,428 --> 00:15:44,204
You're gonna get it.
They're awfully nice people.

181
00:15:44,564 --> 00:15:46,909
A mother, and a
father and a daughter.

182
00:15:46,968 --> 00:15:51,178
You said that before. Look,
son, I want that $32 right now.

183
00:15:51,239 --> 00:15:53,583
If you don't get it, I'll
go and get it myself.

184
00:15:53,641 --> 00:15:55,552
And if I don't get
it, out they go!

185
00:15:55,610 --> 00:15:58,489
But I gave my word of honor
we'd wait a couple of days.

186
00:15:58,546 --> 00:16:01,083
You don't wanna break
your own son's word, do you?

187
00:16:01,148 --> 00:16:05,564
Honor, charity, love thy
neighbor. Everything but money.

188
00:16:05,654 --> 00:16:09,568
Tell me, son, what did money ever
do to you that you hate it so much?

189
00:16:10,726 --> 00:16:11,898
I'll get it.

190
00:16:16,131 --> 00:16:17,201
Oh, hello.

191
00:16:17,266 --> 00:16:19,941
Hello. You forgot
to give me the key.

192
00:16:20,003 --> 00:16:22,415
The key? Yes, the key.

193
00:16:23,039 --> 00:16:24,518
- Here it is.
- Thank you.

194
00:16:24,574 --> 00:16:25,814
Wait a minute.

195
00:16:27,243 --> 00:16:29,621
- You'll stay, won't you?
- If my father...

196
00:16:29,679 --> 00:16:31,056
Who is it, Pete?

197
00:16:33,249 --> 00:16:35,696
Don't go away.
I'll be right back.

198
00:16:38,122 --> 00:16:42,161
- Who was it?
- The postman. It's for you.

199
00:16:44,595 --> 00:16:48,042
National Security Bank.
What do those chiselers want?

200
00:16:53,471 --> 00:16:54,916
What is it?

201
00:16:57,275 --> 00:16:59,585
Just an advertisement.

202
00:17:03,748 --> 00:17:06,320
Katie, come here.

203
00:17:06,652 --> 00:17:07,824
What is it?

204
00:17:07,887 --> 00:17:11,334
That worthless gold mine I
invested in 10 years ago is paying off.

205
00:17:11,390 --> 00:17:13,427
Here's a check for $1,500.

206
00:17:13,492 --> 00:17:16,905
And they say we're gonna get the
same amount each week from now on.

207
00:17:16,963 --> 00:17:21,379
Mercy! It's the day before
Christmas, too. It's money from heaven.

208
00:17:21,435 --> 00:17:24,609
- We should go to church and pray.
- Sure.

209
00:17:24,671 --> 00:17:27,379
But first I'll go down to the
bank and cash the check.

210
00:17:27,441 --> 00:17:30,820
I know that apartment isn't
what you folks have been used to.

211
00:17:31,111 --> 00:17:35,184
- But with a little paint and fixing it up...
- You mean, you'll redecorate it?

212
00:17:37,285 --> 00:17:39,094
I'll speak to my
father about it.

213
00:17:39,153 --> 00:17:42,691
Mother's been having a little
trouble with the stove. It's awfully old.

214
00:17:42,757 --> 00:17:45,169
We'll see what we
can do about that, too.

215
00:17:45,226 --> 00:17:47,866
- Maybe get you another one.
- A new stove!

216
00:17:48,563 --> 00:17:51,203
- Is there somebody asleep in there?
- My father.

217
00:17:52,901 --> 00:17:55,939
My father's a little reluctant
about investing money.

218
00:17:56,105 --> 00:17:58,312
He has to be handled
with kid gloves.

219
00:17:58,574 --> 00:18:02,249
- Say, how about going for a walk?
- Not in these shoes, I wouldn't.

220
00:18:02,311 --> 00:18:05,292
- They pinch my feet.
- Go get some others. I'll wait for you.

221
00:18:05,347 --> 00:18:08,261
It'll be a long wait. I
haven't any others.

222
00:18:08,918 --> 00:18:10,556
What size do you wear?

223
00:18:10,654 --> 00:18:12,861
Dreaming of a size 4. Why?

224
00:18:13,323 --> 00:18:15,303
Stay right where you are.

225
00:18:18,194 --> 00:18:20,401
Not a word of this
to a soul, Katie.

226
00:18:20,463 --> 00:18:22,876
<i>Not anybody, especially
that lazy son of yours.</i>

227
00:18:22,933 --> 00:18:26,210
<i>If he finds out, he'll blow that
blasted tuba the rest of his life.</i>

228
00:18:26,270 --> 00:18:28,876
- Mom, what size shoe do you wear?
- 6, why?

229
00:18:28,973 --> 00:18:30,247
Never mind.

230
00:18:30,307 --> 00:18:34,255
Don't you buy shoes for me for
Christmas. I've got two pairs already.

231
00:18:35,579 --> 00:18:39,289
Isn't that nice? Not a nickel in his
pocket, and he wants to buy me shoes.

232
00:18:39,351 --> 00:18:42,560
Just for that, Frank, I want you
to buy a nice necktie for him...

233
00:18:42,621 --> 00:18:46,125
and put it under the Christmas tree.
He likes those hand-painted ones.

234
00:18:46,191 --> 00:18:48,831
I saw some perfectly
lovely ones for only $5.

235
00:18:49,895 --> 00:18:50,999
$5, eh?

236
00:18:51,063 --> 00:18:54,443
I'll get some paint out of
the attic and paint one myself.

237
00:18:58,738 --> 00:19:01,685
They called us the Amendola
Trio, the Human Pyramid.

238
00:19:02,008 --> 00:19:03,749
Papa balanced Mama
on his shoulders...

239
00:19:03,810 --> 00:19:06,848
and then I stood on top of
Mama, all done up like a little angel.

240
00:19:06,913 --> 00:19:08,017
Sounds exciting.

241
00:19:08,081 --> 00:19:10,722
Papa juggled, Mama
sang, and I played the harp.

242
00:19:10,784 --> 00:19:12,127
Used to go over big.

243
00:19:12,186 --> 00:19:14,666
Only I started growing,
Mama got heavier...

244
00:19:14,722 --> 00:19:17,202
and one day,
Papa's legs gave out.

245
00:19:17,291 --> 00:19:18,770
You mean he dropped you?

246
00:19:18,826 --> 00:19:22,535
Four times in one week. The
fourth time, Mama broke her leg.

247
00:19:22,596 --> 00:19:24,371
You gotta hand
it to Papa, though.

248
00:19:24,431 --> 00:19:27,276
He never broke a plate.
He's a great juggler.

249
00:19:28,002 --> 00:19:30,983
Look, there's my father,
coming out of the bank.

250
00:19:40,148 --> 00:19:43,289
- Wonder what he was doing in the bank.
- I've never been in a bank.

251
00:19:43,352 --> 00:19:45,491
Just to think of all that
money in one place.

252
00:19:45,554 --> 00:19:49,400
A week ago, this tree was $7.
Yesterday, $4. Now, what are my bids?

253
00:19:49,458 --> 00:19:51,028
- 40 cents.
- Make it $1.

254
00:19:51,093 --> 00:19:52,094
One dollar.

255
00:19:52,161 --> 00:19:54,334
I wish we were
gonna have a tree.

256
00:19:54,396 --> 00:19:56,343
Of course, with
the moving and all...

257
00:19:56,399 --> 00:19:59,039
We've talked enough about
me. Tell me, where do you work?

258
00:19:59,102 --> 00:20:01,104
I don't. That is, I write music.

259
00:20:01,171 --> 00:20:02,946
- Does it pay?
- No.

260
00:20:03,006 --> 00:20:04,314
Then you don't work.

261
00:20:04,374 --> 00:20:06,445
- I don't have a job.
- Can't you find one?

262
00:20:06,510 --> 00:20:08,786
- I've never looked for one.
- What about money?

263
00:20:08,845 --> 00:20:10,256
I don't need money.

264
00:20:10,313 --> 00:20:14,126
Suppose you were walking with a
girl, and she wanted a malted milk.

265
00:20:14,185 --> 00:20:15,630
What would you do?

266
00:20:15,686 --> 00:20:18,223
Well, that would be
pretty embarrassing.

267
00:20:20,091 --> 00:20:22,071
I'm sorry I said that, Pete.

268
00:20:22,126 --> 00:20:23,469
Come on, let's go home.

269
00:20:25,396 --> 00:20:28,310
Folks, just two more left.
What am I bid for this one?

270
00:20:28,367 --> 00:20:30,176
- 40 cents.
- $1.50.

271
00:20:30,235 --> 00:20:31,873
$1.50. Do I hear $2?

272
00:20:31,937 --> 00:20:35,612
<i>$1.50, once, twice. Sold
to this gentleman for $1.50!</i>

273
00:20:35,908 --> 00:20:39,481
Now for the last tree. It's your last
chance. What're my bids for this one?

274
00:20:39,545 --> 00:20:41,456
- 40 cents.
- 75 cents.

275
00:20:41,513 --> 00:20:43,790
Do I hear $1? Will
someone make it $1?

276
00:20:43,850 --> 00:20:46,353
- 75 cents, once, twice...
- $1.

277
00:20:46,419 --> 00:20:49,332
$1, I've got. Once, twice,
sold to this gentleman for $1.

278
00:20:49,389 --> 00:20:50,732
Here you are, buddy.

279
00:20:50,790 --> 00:20:54,237
Say, mister, would it be all
right if I pay you 40 cents now...

280
00:20:54,694 --> 00:20:56,071
and the balance later?

281
00:20:56,129 --> 00:21:00,010
Sure! Just take the elevator to the
18th floor. That's our credit department.

282
00:21:00,067 --> 00:21:03,844
This is a cash sale, buddy. Do
you want the tree or don't you?

283
00:21:07,308 --> 00:21:08,514
Hey, wait!

284
00:21:09,844 --> 00:21:12,450
Look, you can have the
tree. Give me the 40 cents.

285
00:21:12,513 --> 00:21:14,118
Thanks!

286
00:21:14,182 --> 00:21:16,685
I'll pay you the rest
as soon as I can.

287
00:21:16,751 --> 00:21:19,254
I'll give you a week. If
you don't pay by then...

288
00:21:19,321 --> 00:21:22,131
I'll find out where you
live and take the tree back.

289
00:21:25,594 --> 00:21:27,301
...11, 12...

290
00:21:27,562 --> 00:21:30,100
13, 14, 15.

291
00:21:30,166 --> 00:21:33,340
- Why didn't you leave it at the bank?
- At the bank?

292
00:21:33,469 --> 00:21:34,709
Katie, you're not serious.

293
00:21:34,770 --> 00:21:38,047
After all that happened to
our little savings in 1929?

294
00:21:38,474 --> 00:21:42,081
No, sirree, they're never gonna
get any of my money again.

295
00:21:42,278 --> 00:21:45,419
I'm going to hide it
right in this house.

296
00:21:49,419 --> 00:21:52,730
You better run over to the church
and say that little prayer now.

297
00:21:52,789 --> 00:21:54,427
You're sending me away...

298
00:21:54,491 --> 00:21:57,301
because you want to keep the
hiding place a secret from me.

299
00:21:57,361 --> 00:21:59,500
No, it isn't you,
Katie. It's Pete.

300
00:21:59,563 --> 00:22:02,238
You're too soft-hearted
with that boy.

301
00:22:02,533 --> 00:22:05,844
- I'm afraid that...
- What's the use of money if you hide it?

302
00:22:05,904 --> 00:22:07,542
Money's for buying things...

303
00:22:07,605 --> 00:22:09,744
to enjoy life, to
have some pleasure.

304
00:22:09,807 --> 00:22:12,151
That's a lot of hooey.
Money's for security...

305
00:22:12,210 --> 00:22:14,690
not to worry about the
future, to enjoy sleep.

306
00:22:14,746 --> 00:22:16,282
All right, Frank, I'm going.

307
00:22:16,347 --> 00:22:18,851
I don't care if you stick
that money up the chimney...

308
00:22:18,918 --> 00:22:21,990
and let it smoke till it
smells like a ham butt.

309
00:23:02,697 --> 00:23:04,837
How long is it going to be?

310
00:23:05,100 --> 00:23:07,307
Moving from one
place to another.

311
00:23:07,369 --> 00:23:09,178
Always moving...

312
00:23:09,237 --> 00:23:11,945
never having a
place to call our home.

313
00:23:13,141 --> 00:23:15,951
Believe me, Lord,
we've tried so hard.

314
00:23:16,979 --> 00:23:20,018
We've tried
everything. Everything.

315
00:23:22,819 --> 00:23:26,961
But it's so difficult to find
a job for a human pyramid.

316
00:23:29,625 --> 00:23:31,468
You're our only hope.

317
00:23:31,828 --> 00:23:33,569
So it's up to you.

318
00:23:35,532 --> 00:23:36,704
Please.

319
00:25:03,258 --> 00:25:05,169
Tomorrow is Christmas.

320
00:25:06,061 --> 00:25:08,235
We haven't even got a tree.

321
00:25:09,265 --> 00:25:11,802
No money to buy
some little gifts.

322
00:25:13,269 --> 00:25:16,011
Not even a pair of
shoes for Rosalinda.

323
00:25:22,779 --> 00:25:25,386
She must have those shoes, Lord.

324
00:25:26,350 --> 00:25:28,489
She needs them real bad.

325
00:25:47,405 --> 00:25:48,816
Thank you!

326
00:26:36,858 --> 00:26:38,394
Tell me, Rosalinda...

327
00:26:38,526 --> 00:26:40,836
- do you still play the harp?
- Sure, why?

328
00:26:41,162 --> 00:26:44,610
I think I'll compose something
just for the harp and tuba.

329
00:26:44,667 --> 00:26:48,171
<i>And I'm gonna</i> call <i>it The Melody
Of Two Orphaned Instruments.</i>

330
00:26:48,237 --> 00:26:49,739
That's a cute title.

331
00:26:49,972 --> 00:26:52,509
- You think they'll go together?
- I don't know.

332
00:26:52,575 --> 00:26:54,179
But I'm hoping.

333
00:27:19,737 --> 00:27:21,944
Papa! Wait!

334
00:27:34,886 --> 00:27:36,866
But I don't understand.

335
00:27:37,222 --> 00:27:38,929
A tree! Presents!

336
00:27:38,991 --> 00:27:40,834
And look at it, Papa, a turkey!

337
00:27:40,892 --> 00:27:44,670
And cranberry sauce, plum
pudding, candy, and nuts.

338
00:27:44,897 --> 00:27:46,899
<i>And, look, brand-new shoes.</i>

339
00:27:48,067 --> 00:27:49,944
But how? I don't understand.

340
00:27:50,103 --> 00:27:52,242
You couldn't have won
it on a quiz program.

341
00:27:52,305 --> 00:27:55,411
- We haven't got any phone.
- No, Papa, I didn't win anything.

342
00:27:55,475 --> 00:27:58,285
Mama! Your wedding
ring! You didn't hock your...

343
00:27:58,344 --> 00:28:01,326
- No, I didn't hock anything. All I did...
- I know.

344
00:28:01,715 --> 00:28:04,525
You wired that drummer in
Boston who had a crush on you.{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boston}

345
00:28:04,585 --> 00:28:07,464
That sheik with the patent
leather shoes and tuxedo.

346
00:28:07,521 --> 00:28:10,798
I remember what he said when
we announced our engagement.

347
00:28:11,091 --> 00:28:12,764
"If ever you needed anything- ♪

348
00:28:12,826 --> 00:28:15,307
Papa, that was 22 years ago.

349
00:28:15,363 --> 00:28:17,775
I don't care if it
was 100 years ago!

350
00:28:17,832 --> 00:28:20,039
I never trusted a
guy with a tuxedo...

351
00:28:20,101 --> 00:28:21,671
especially a rented one.

352
00:28:21,736 --> 00:28:23,409
Let Mama explain, Papa.

353
00:28:23,638 --> 00:28:25,618
No, the money
didn't come from him.

354
00:28:25,674 --> 00:28:27,483
It came from...

355
00:28:27,642 --> 00:28:30,020
Here, Papa, you'd
better sit down.

356
00:28:32,715 --> 00:28:36,720
- Better loosen your collar, Papa.
- Rosalinda, bring Papa a glass of water.

357
00:28:38,587 --> 00:28:41,033
Papa, you love me, don't you?

358
00:28:41,524 --> 00:28:44,027
- Of course I love you.
- And you trust me?

359
00:28:44,393 --> 00:28:47,034
You'd believe me if I told
you something, wouldn't you?

360
00:28:47,097 --> 00:28:50,306
Even if nobody else in the
world would believe me...

361
00:28:50,434 --> 00:28:52,914
you would believe me,
wouldn't you, Papa?

362
00:28:52,969 --> 00:28:54,778
Sure, I would, Mama.

363
00:28:56,340 --> 00:28:59,446
I was sitting right
here in this chair...

364
00:29:00,143 --> 00:29:01,851
and I was praying.

365
00:29:06,384 --> 00:29:07,988
This money...

366
00:29:08,486 --> 00:29:10,989
$1,500..

367
00:29:11,222 --> 00:29:14,965
Come floating right down
through the hole in the skylight.

368
00:29:15,827 --> 00:29:17,739
$1.500?

369
00:29:18,697 --> 00:29:21,837
- From heaven?
- You do believe me, don't you, Papa?

370
00:29:22,634 --> 00:29:25,444
If you said it, I
gotta believe you.

371
00:29:26,672 --> 00:29:29,243
Merry Christmas, Papa.

372
00:29:29,341 --> 00:29:31,116
Merry Christmas, Papa.

373
00:29:32,478 --> 00:29:36,188
You see, Rosie? I told you
old Saint Nick wouldn't forget us.

374
00:29:36,249 --> 00:29:38,525
All we needed was an address.

375
00:29:41,287 --> 00:29:44,325
Frank, this perfume
is lovely. "Dangerous."

376
00:29:44,824 --> 00:29:48,033
Such a big bottle. It must
have cost at least $10.

377
00:29:48,094 --> 00:29:50,075
It's not that dangerous.

378
00:29:50,131 --> 00:29:52,873
This is a terrific tie,
Dad. Thanks again.

379
00:29:53,200 --> 00:29:56,010
Did you see the watermelons,
Mama? They're hand-painted.

380
00:29:57,204 --> 00:29:58,808
Now, who's that?

381
00:30:03,677 --> 00:30:06,784
Merry Christmas, young
man. May we come in?

382
00:30:06,848 --> 00:30:08,327
Why, of course.

383
00:30:08,383 --> 00:30:10,488
<i>Merry Christmas.</i>

384
00:30:11,019 --> 00:30:12,999
Merry Christmas to you.

385
00:30:16,725 --> 00:30:20,469
Why, Mr. Amendola, we
have the exact same ties on.

386
00:30:20,563 --> 00:30:24,170
It could be a lot worse if we were
girls wearing the same dresses.

387
00:30:26,869 --> 00:30:30,146
Mom, Dad, this is
Mr. and Mrs. Amendola...

388
00:30:30,206 --> 00:30:32,584
- and their daughter, Rosalinda.
- How do you do?

389
00:30:32,642 --> 00:30:36,250
We're your new tenants, and the
Yuletide season being upon us...

390
00:30:36,313 --> 00:30:38,725
we thought we'd drop
in and pay you a visit.

391
00:30:38,782 --> 00:30:40,989
- To pay what?
- A visit, Frank.

392
00:30:42,986 --> 00:30:44,260
Make yourselves comfortable.

393
00:30:44,321 --> 00:30:46,198
- Do sit down.
- Thanks.

394
00:30:46,256 --> 00:30:49,829
- I'm glad you brought your harp.
- Can we put it right over here?

395
00:30:49,893 --> 00:30:52,500
That's a nice tree you
got there, Mr. Dingle.

396
00:30:52,564 --> 00:30:54,805
Where did you
get it? Molineri's?

397
00:30:54,866 --> 00:30:58,404
Maybe we ought to go down there next
week and make our payments together.

398
00:30:58,470 --> 00:31:00,950
Payments? Speaking
of payments...

399
00:31:01,005 --> 00:31:02,814
just what do you
do for a living?

400
00:31:02,874 --> 00:31:05,855
Doesn't the name of Amendola
suggest nothing to you?

401
00:31:05,910 --> 00:31:08,892
You surely must have
seen us perform some place.

402
00:31:08,948 --> 00:31:12,725
Florida in the racing season,
Lake Placid in the winter season...

403
00:31:12,785 --> 00:31:15,425
or perhaps Africa in
the malaria season.

404
00:31:15,488 --> 00:31:19,959
Yes, sir, someday you'll be able to
say the Amendolas were your tenants.

405
00:31:20,226 --> 00:31:23,731
If I don't get my rent money,
I'll be able to say that tomorrow.

406
00:31:23,797 --> 00:31:26,403
I'm glad you brought
that up, Mr. Dingle.

407
00:31:26,466 --> 00:31:29,606
Now, if you'll just hang
your sock on the mantle...

408
00:31:29,669 --> 00:31:32,343
I'll drop in three months' rent.

409
00:31:32,672 --> 00:31:34,515
Keep the $4 change.

410
00:31:34,908 --> 00:31:37,218
Three months' rent in advance.

411
00:31:37,712 --> 00:31:41,558
Mr. Amendola, we're so glad
you folks dropped in on us.

412
00:31:41,782 --> 00:31:43,853
Katie, get
Mr. Amendola a chair...

413
00:31:43,918 --> 00:31:46,125
and some coffee and cake.

414
00:31:46,187 --> 00:31:48,030
<i>Where's the Christmas spirit?</i>

415
00:31:48,089 --> 00:31:50,763
That's right, where is
the Christmas spirit?

416
00:32:06,909 --> 00:32:09,481
I love the excitement
of Christmas.

417
00:32:10,446 --> 00:32:13,552
I'll never forget one Christmas
Eve when I was a kid.

418
00:32:13,983 --> 00:32:16,486
I hung my stocking up on
the mantle and went to bed.

419
00:32:16,553 --> 00:32:20,194
When I woke up the next morning,
what do you think I found in the stocking?

420
00:32:20,256 --> 00:32:21,963
My father's foot!

421
00:32:26,864 --> 00:32:27,865
Soft.

422
00:32:30,401 --> 00:32:31,505
Soft.

423
00:32:33,904 --> 00:32:35,315
Wait a minute!

424
00:32:35,673 --> 00:32:37,812
Another outburst
like that, Mr. Dingle...

425
00:32:37,875 --> 00:32:40,186
and I'll hide your
electric trains.

426
00:32:44,149 --> 00:32:45,184
Soft.

427
00:32:47,886 --> 00:32:49,024
Soft!

428
00:32:50,789 --> 00:32:54,293
How do you like that? There's a
guy going in business for himself.

429
00:32:55,527 --> 00:32:57,769
Wait a minute. All together!

430
00:33:16,550 --> 00:33:18,530
This is wonderful.

431
00:34:05,135 --> 00:34:09,174
Mr. Dingle, this is gonna develop
into a long and beautiful friendship...

432
00:34:09,239 --> 00:34:11,776
even closer than
Damon and Phythenias.

433
00:34:23,788 --> 00:34:26,325
You'll notice Rosalinda
brought her harp along.

434
00:34:26,390 --> 00:34:29,270
She didn't bring it to
slice hard-boiled eggs.

435
00:34:29,327 --> 00:34:31,068
All right, Rosalinda.

436
00:34:31,129 --> 00:34:33,871
- Here you are, Rosalinda.
- Thank you.

437
00:34:33,932 --> 00:34:36,242
<i>I finished The Melody for
Two Orphaned Instruments.</i>

438
00:34:36,301 --> 00:34:39,771
- Wanna try it?
- Sure. Set the music right over here, Pete.

439
00:34:40,739 --> 00:34:42,650
- Here you are.
- Thanks.

440
00:34:47,814 --> 00:34:49,293
- Ready?
- Yes.

441
00:35:30,291 --> 00:35:32,465
I don't want my
wife to hear this.

442
00:35:32,862 --> 00:35:35,206
The stage has
always fascinated me.

443
00:35:35,264 --> 00:35:38,074
Don't you think I've got
a very fine singing voice?

444
00:35:38,134 --> 00:35:41,172
Why, the bathtubs of the world
are full of singers, Mr. Dingle.

445
00:35:41,237 --> 00:35:45,413
But did you ever hear of anyone
doing a backflip in the bathtub? No.

446
00:35:45,474 --> 00:35:47,284
And you know the reason why?

447
00:35:47,344 --> 00:35:49,415
Because only a few
of us are acrobats.

448
00:35:49,479 --> 00:35:52,892
Acrobats. I'd just love
to learn a few tricks.

449
00:35:52,949 --> 00:35:55,862
I'm always so dull when
company comes. I can't do a thing.

450
00:35:55,919 --> 00:35:58,297
Can you teach me a few things?

451
00:35:58,355 --> 00:36:00,596
I'd be glad to teach you.

452
00:36:00,657 --> 00:36:04,470
You got anything around here that
we could rig up like a teeterboard?

453
00:36:04,829 --> 00:36:06,433
- A seesaw?
- Yeah.

454
00:36:06,864 --> 00:36:09,470
I think so. Let me see, now.

455
00:36:11,068 --> 00:36:14,140
- How about a table leaf?
- You got one? Go get it.

456
00:36:23,615 --> 00:36:26,095
Great, now get
me a glass of water.

457
00:36:34,260 --> 00:36:35,466
Thanks.

458
00:36:35,528 --> 00:36:39,840
This is so simple, I taught it to
Rosalinda when she was only 3 years old.

459
00:36:39,899 --> 00:36:41,810
Now you get up on that chair.

460
00:36:42,135 --> 00:36:45,742
I'll stand down at this end, place
the glass of water on my head...

461
00:36:45,805 --> 00:36:48,479
and when I say go, you
jump down on that end.

462
00:36:48,908 --> 00:36:52,186
I will then do a back somersault,
light a cigarette in midair...

463
00:36:52,246 --> 00:36:55,386
and land in that chair without
spilling one drop of water.

464
00:36:55,449 --> 00:36:57,019
- Ready?
- Ready.

465
00:37:00,254 --> 00:37:01,255
Go.

466
00:37:08,096 --> 00:37:09,097
Termites.

467
00:37:31,654 --> 00:37:34,863
That's so pretty, Pete.
And he wrote it himself.

468
00:37:35,491 --> 00:37:37,836
How well he plays the tuba!

469
00:37:38,595 --> 00:37:41,201
- It's lovely, Pete.
- So are you.

470
00:37:42,132 --> 00:37:44,942
Gosh, Rosalinda,
your hair, your dress...

471
00:37:45,502 --> 00:37:48,108
- Gosh, you're beautiful.
- Thank you, Pete.

472
00:37:48,438 --> 00:37:50,884
- May I have this, Pete?
- Sure.

473
00:37:51,308 --> 00:37:52,686
I wrote it for you.

474
00:37:56,814 --> 00:38:00,591
Look, somebody's coming
to see us, in a beautiful car.

475
00:38:04,989 --> 00:38:08,767
Rosalinda, go down and see who it
is. It might be somebody important.

476
00:38:17,469 --> 00:38:21,076
- Yes?
- Hello. Is Joe Mahoney home?

477
00:38:21,140 --> 00:38:23,178
- He's moved away.
- He has?

478
00:38:23,643 --> 00:38:26,317
- You know where he went?
- I'm afraid I don't.

479
00:38:26,379 --> 00:38:30,293
That's great. What do I do with
this Christmas present I brought him?

480
00:38:31,718 --> 00:38:33,391
Here, it's yours.

481
00:38:33,954 --> 00:38:35,695
- Go ahead.
- I really don't...

482
00:38:35,755 --> 00:38:37,632
It's just fruit and things.

483
00:38:38,425 --> 00:38:40,099
- You live here?
- Yes.

484
00:38:40,161 --> 00:38:42,107
- Alone?
- With my parents.

485
00:38:42,163 --> 00:38:43,836
That's nice.

486
00:38:46,701 --> 00:38:49,375
- Anything I can do for you?
- My name is Phil Davis.

487
00:38:49,437 --> 00:38:52,350
- I'm a friend of Joe Mahoney's...
- Phil Davis, the agent?

488
00:38:52,406 --> 00:38:55,752
- Yes, that's me.
- Believe me, this is a pleasure.

489
00:38:55,844 --> 00:38:59,189
My name is Amendola. You've heard
of the Amendola Trio, haven't you?

490
00:38:59,247 --> 00:39:01,955
You know, the Human
Pyramid? Come in.

491
00:39:08,523 --> 00:39:11,937
Now, don't pay any attention
to this apartment, Mr. Davis.

492
00:39:11,995 --> 00:39:15,204
We just moved in yesterday.
Not much of a place...

493
00:39:15,398 --> 00:39:18,971
but for certain reasons
it fascinates me.

494
00:39:19,602 --> 00:39:23,448
<i>Mr. Davis, take a
look at this poster.</i>

495
00:39:24,240 --> 00:39:27,814
You're gazing at one of the
greatest vaudeville acts of its time.

496
00:39:28,345 --> 00:39:30,825
And the only reason
we disbanded...

497
00:39:30,881 --> 00:39:33,487
was because of my little
daughter's rapid maturity.

498
00:39:33,550 --> 00:39:38,226
- Yes, I can see what you mean.
- Mr. Davis, I was just thinking...

499
00:39:38,355 --> 00:39:42,065
I can work alone. Why, I can
out-juggle anyone in the business.

500
00:39:42,127 --> 00:39:45,506
You know something? I can
juggle 25 plates with one hand.

501
00:39:45,697 --> 00:39:48,803
And with the other hand,
I pick up the broken ones.

502
00:39:50,502 --> 00:39:52,709
Just a little joke
I use in the act.

503
00:39:53,271 --> 00:39:55,945
Now if you give me
your undivided attention...

504
00:40:25,972 --> 00:40:29,921
Say, that's good. That's
great. How did you do it?

505
00:40:31,212 --> 00:40:32,623
<i>How did you do it?</i>

506
00:40:33,214 --> 00:40:36,525
What? That's a trade secret.

507
00:40:36,951 --> 00:40:39,397
- Do it again.
- Yes, do it again, Papa.

508
00:40:39,454 --> 00:40:43,403
Rosalinda, you know the great
Amendola never repeats twice.

509
00:40:44,426 --> 00:40:47,930
That's very clever. I'll try
and book you, Mr. Amendola.

510
00:40:48,063 --> 00:40:49,599
Sure.

511
00:40:51,400 --> 00:40:53,346
- How about you?
- About me?

512
00:40:55,304 --> 00:40:58,342
I'm sorry to butt in,
but the coffee's ready.

513
00:40:59,309 --> 00:41:02,188
A girl with your looks and
figure should be in the limelight.

514
00:41:02,245 --> 00:41:04,350
And I'm just the man
that can put you there.

515
00:41:04,414 --> 00:41:08,089
Said he as he twirled his black
mustache and eyed the proud beauty.

516
00:41:08,151 --> 00:41:09,824
- Who's he?
- Please.

517
00:41:10,220 --> 00:41:11,893
You were saying, Mr. Davis?

518
00:41:11,955 --> 00:41:14,960
A girl like you should
have a decent place to live.

519
00:41:15,559 --> 00:41:17,004
Not a dump like this.

520
00:41:17,061 --> 00:41:18,836
What's good enough
for my parents...

521
00:41:18,896 --> 00:41:21,638
Is hardly good enough
for a raving beauty like you.

522
00:41:21,699 --> 00:41:23,975
- And Mr. Davis here is just...
- Please.

523
00:41:24,468 --> 00:41:27,745
The coffee's ready. It's
getting cold, and so is the cake.

524
00:41:27,805 --> 00:41:32,016
I'm sorry, but Mr. Davis is a
very important Broadway agent.

525
00:41:32,244 --> 00:41:36,056
- Broadway chiseler.
- I beg your pardon. What was that?

526
00:41:36,181 --> 00:41:37,626
I know the type.

527
00:41:37,682 --> 00:41:41,528
Promises young girls expensive
clothes, diamonds, furs.

528
00:41:41,786 --> 00:41:45,199
- Anything to make an impression.
- He didn't say that. You did.

529
00:41:45,257 --> 00:41:46,828
He was going to.

530
00:41:50,096 --> 00:41:53,441
What right has he got to call me a
chiseler? He doesn't even know me.

531
00:41:53,499 --> 00:41:55,035
I think he's jealous.

532
00:41:57,069 --> 00:41:58,878
Maybe he's got a case.

533
00:41:59,138 --> 00:42:01,949
Say, it's Christmas,
and I'm all alone.

534
00:42:02,843 --> 00:42:05,414
How about me taking you
out to a Chinese dinner tonight?

535
00:42:05,479 --> 00:42:07,117
I'm sorry, but I was...

536
00:42:08,081 --> 00:42:09,685
I'd love to.

537
00:42:10,317 --> 00:42:12,923
Father, may I go
out with Mr. Davis?

538
00:42:13,353 --> 00:42:16,232
- You better ask your mother.
- Thanks a lot.

539
00:42:19,961 --> 00:42:22,965
I threw five nuts up,
and only four came down.

540
00:42:23,731 --> 00:42:26,075
This defies the laws of gravity.

541
00:42:49,559 --> 00:42:52,233
It's midnight. She
isn't home yet.

542
00:42:52,662 --> 00:42:55,541
Mama, stop worrying.
Nothing can happen to her.

543
00:42:55,799 --> 00:42:57,972
She's not alone.
She's with a man.

544
00:42:59,769 --> 00:43:02,215
Another miracle
happened this afternoon.

545
00:43:02,272 --> 00:43:05,618
- A miracle?
- I tried to juggle a few walnuts.

546
00:43:05,743 --> 00:43:09,486
- One didn't come down. It just disappeared.
- Mercy me.

547
00:43:09,714 --> 00:43:13,821
This is a strange house. Money
comes down and walnuts go up.

548
00:43:14,452 --> 00:43:18,264
But I guess that's better than money
going up and walnuts coming down.

549
00:43:19,258 --> 00:43:21,602
I don't know what to make of it.

550
00:43:42,048 --> 00:43:44,119
Katie, Katie!

551
00:43:45,618 --> 00:43:47,063
What is it, Frank?

552
00:43:48,054 --> 00:43:50,592
I've been thinking about
that Amendola family.

553
00:43:50,658 --> 00:43:53,696
Why should people who can
afford three months' rent in advance...

554
00:43:53,761 --> 00:43:55,798
wanna live in a dump like that?

555
00:43:55,863 --> 00:43:59,811
Frank, last night you couldn't sleep
because they didn't pay their rent.

556
00:43:59,967 --> 00:44:02,413
Tonight you can't
sleep because they did.

557
00:44:03,237 --> 00:44:06,048
<i>For goodness' sake, stop
worrying and go to sleep.</i>

558
00:44:35,104 --> 00:44:38,109
Why don't you check out of
this town? Come to New York.{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_York_City}

559
00:44:38,175 --> 00:44:41,884
- Things can happen to you there, Rosie.
- Let's not start that again, Phil.

560
00:44:41,945 --> 00:44:45,154
I'm crazy about you,
Rosie. I'll do anything for you.

561
00:44:46,183 --> 00:44:49,653
Do you know any
music publishers, Phil?

562
00:44:50,754 --> 00:44:52,894
Sure, dozens of them. Why?

563
00:44:52,957 --> 00:44:57,030
I wrote some music. I think it's
beautiful. If you could take it...

564
00:44:57,095 --> 00:44:59,200
I didn't know you
were a composer.

565
00:45:01,866 --> 00:45:05,245
<i>"Melody for Two Orphaned
Instruments by Peter Dingle."</i>

566
00:45:05,570 --> 00:45:07,174
Who's Peter Dingle?

567
00:45:07,238 --> 00:45:09,651
You see, I put a
man's name on it...

568
00:45:09,708 --> 00:45:13,212
because I knew no one would even
look at it if they knew a girl wrote it.

569
00:45:13,278 --> 00:45:16,691
I'll see what I can do. If it's any
good, I'll try and get it published.

570
00:45:16,749 --> 00:45:21,027
- Thanks a lot, Phil. Good night.
- Wait a minute.

571
00:45:22,187 --> 00:45:23,791
See you tomorrow.

572
00:46:11,941 --> 00:46:15,821
- I'm sorry to wake you up, Mr. Haggerty.
- What's the matter, Pete? Somebody sick?

573
00:46:15,878 --> 00:46:18,256
Yeah, me. I want that job.

574
00:46:19,982 --> 00:46:23,759
Do you want to start right now, or
can you wait till tomorrow morning?

575
00:46:24,120 --> 00:46:26,931
I'm sorry, I was afraid
somebody else might get it.

576
00:46:26,990 --> 00:46:29,698
You see, Mr. Haggerty,
I need money.

577
00:46:39,670 --> 00:46:40,671
Hey, Pete.

578
00:46:41,371 --> 00:46:42,373
Yeah?

579
00:46:48,112 --> 00:46:52,026
Bend closer, laddie boy.
This is for your ears alone.

580
00:46:53,051 --> 00:46:55,930
I got a sure thing in the
sixth at California today.

581
00:46:55,987 --> 00:46:58,764
Cut me in on 40 percent,
and I'll give it to you.

582
00:46:58,824 --> 00:47:00,963
<i>This one can't lose.
It'll win in a walk.</i>

583
00:47:01,026 --> 00:47:03,939
And while our horse is walking,
the others will be running.

584
00:47:03,996 --> 00:47:07,534
If you have another hot tip,
Mulligan, leave it under the plate.

585
00:47:13,406 --> 00:47:15,818
- Yes, sir?
- What's your best cigar?

586
00:47:15,876 --> 00:47:19,323
- Good ones are a quarter apiece.
- Quarter apiece?

587
00:47:19,646 --> 00:47:21,853
<i>How much does a whole one cost?</i>

588
00:47:23,183 --> 00:47:26,357
- It's just a little joke.
- As little as I've heard.

589
00:47:28,355 --> 00:47:29,357
Pete.

590
00:47:32,260 --> 00:47:33,637
- Hello.
- Hi.

591
00:47:34,695 --> 00:47:36,140
What will it be?

592
00:47:38,332 --> 00:47:42,974
- I didn't know you worked here, Pete.
- That's strange. It was in the papers.

593
00:47:43,070 --> 00:47:46,211
But remember, once you told
me you were opposed to work.

594
00:47:46,375 --> 00:47:47,410
Sure.

595
00:47:47,809 --> 00:47:51,916
I even remember I couldn't buy a
certain girl a cool drink in a drugstore.

596
00:47:52,180 --> 00:47:56,026
Now, not only can I buy it,
but serve it, too. What will it be?

597
00:47:58,086 --> 00:48:00,431
You took this job
on account of me.

598
00:48:00,823 --> 00:48:04,532
The girl I'm referring to
only wanted a malted milk.

599
00:48:06,229 --> 00:48:07,537
Can we go now?

600
00:48:08,131 --> 00:48:11,977
- It's you. Let's go, honey.
- I'd like a malted milk, Phil.

601
00:48:12,168 --> 00:48:14,944
- Not in this dump, please.
- Goodbye.

602
00:48:16,807 --> 00:48:17,808
So long.

603
00:48:21,245 --> 00:48:25,421
Pete, you're missing a great bid.
That filly's sure to wind up in the money.

604
00:48:25,549 --> 00:48:27,586
Yeah, that's what I'm afraid of.

605
00:48:43,969 --> 00:48:46,780
How's that for a
bargain? Only $49.50.

606
00:48:46,839 --> 00:48:50,309
But, Papa, what do we
want with a polar bear rug?

607
00:48:50,610 --> 00:48:53,887
This isn't an ordinary
polar bear. Just watch.

608
00:48:54,547 --> 00:48:57,050
All you got to do is
twist the tooth and...

609
00:48:59,819 --> 00:49:01,730
Music! It's also a radio.

610
00:49:02,556 --> 00:49:04,968
The other tooth
is for shortwave.

611
00:49:05,125 --> 00:49:08,663
- But we don't need it.
- Sure we do.

612
00:49:08,962 --> 00:49:11,306
It lends an air of
quality to the place.

613
00:49:11,365 --> 00:49:14,676
Mama, what's a real
home without a polar bear?

614
00:49:14,735 --> 00:49:17,181
But we must stop buying things.

615
00:49:17,237 --> 00:49:20,981
That $500 you have
left has got to last.

616
00:49:22,810 --> 00:49:24,983
I'm glad you brought that up.

617
00:49:25,046 --> 00:49:28,926
You know that Mr. Byfield, who we
bought these few pieces of furniture off?

618
00:49:29,317 --> 00:49:33,129
I stopped by there today to look at
that little piano we were talking about.

619
00:49:33,187 --> 00:49:34,826
And you know something?

620
00:49:35,290 --> 00:49:38,794
- He isn't doing so well.
- Papa, I hope you didn't...

621
00:49:38,861 --> 00:49:41,341
It's not his fault
that business is bad.

622
00:49:41,397 --> 00:49:43,638
Naturally, with the banks
leaning on the place...

623
00:49:43,699 --> 00:49:45,736
- and no one to...
- Our last $500.

624
00:49:46,201 --> 00:49:49,308
Papa, all our lives,
you've been doing this.

625
00:49:49,439 --> 00:49:53,046
Whenever we get a little ahead,
you meet someone who's in trouble...

626
00:49:53,109 --> 00:49:56,886
and you just melt like
chocolate in a baby's hand.

627
00:49:57,147 --> 00:49:59,627
I couldn't help
it. He needed it.

628
00:50:00,216 --> 00:50:04,927
But, Mama, we got nothing to worry about.
We still got that $100 I gave you yesterday.

629
00:50:04,989 --> 00:50:09,870
No, I mailed that money away for
the unpaid bills we left in Chicago.

630
00:50:09,960 --> 00:50:12,770
That puts us right back
where we were a week ago.

631
00:50:13,597 --> 00:50:17,443
That's right. All you gotta do
is do what you did a week ago.

632
00:50:17,835 --> 00:50:18,836
Pray.

633
00:50:18,936 --> 00:50:21,349
Lam praying every
hour of the day.

634
00:50:21,406 --> 00:50:23,784
But if it's more money
you want me to pray for...

635
00:50:23,842 --> 00:50:26,186
Why not, Mother?
It's worth a try.

636
00:50:31,016 --> 00:50:33,929
I just wouldn't have
the nerve to ask again.

637
00:50:34,286 --> 00:50:36,267
Besides, it's selfish.

638
00:50:36,956 --> 00:50:39,596
But Papa just told us he
helped somebody with it.

639
00:50:39,659 --> 00:50:41,161
That wasn't selfish.

640
00:50:41,360 --> 00:50:46,036
And besides, all you gotta do is ask,
and let heaven make up its own mind.

641
00:50:46,532 --> 00:50:50,309
- Even if I could remember the prayer...
- You must remember, Mother.

642
00:50:50,369 --> 00:50:53,817
- The same thing you said before.
- I don't know.

643
00:50:54,041 --> 00:50:57,250
All I recall saying is that
Rosalinda needs shoes.

644
00:50:57,311 --> 00:51:00,781
But now she doesn't need
shoes. She has four pairs already.

645
00:51:00,848 --> 00:51:03,727
But, Mama, when you
say she needs shoes...

646
00:51:03,784 --> 00:51:06,094
that doesn't mean
she really needs them.

647
00:51:06,153 --> 00:51:09,897
It's like stagehands in the
theater playing dice. A guy says:

648
00:51:09,958 --> 00:51:12,234
"Baby needs a
new pair of shoes."

649
00:51:12,293 --> 00:51:16,708
That doesn't mean she needs them.
It's just a disfigurement of speech.

650
00:51:17,432 --> 00:51:20,743
- All right, bring me the little old rocker.
<i>- I'll</i> get it.

651
00:51:20,802 --> 00:51:23,113
I was sitting in it
when it happened.

652
00:51:30,679 --> 00:51:32,522
Say it, Mama.

653
00:51:34,416 --> 00:51:38,763
Please, help us. Rosalinda needs
shoes. She needs them real bad.

654
00:51:57,308 --> 00:52:00,983
It's no use, Mama. The
prayer won't be answered.

655
00:52:02,380 --> 00:52:05,850
Maybe it has been answered,
and the answer is no.

656
00:52:11,590 --> 00:52:13,194
<i>We're grateful, anyhow.</i>

657
00:52:14,159 --> 00:52:17,333
<i>I guess I'd better take
this rug back to the shop.</i>

658
00:52:28,141 --> 00:52:30,621
Please forgive
me for telling a lie.

659
00:52:31,444 --> 00:52:33,981
Rosalinda doesn't need shoes.

660
00:52:44,425 --> 00:52:47,429
<i>Come back! It's coming down!</i>

661
00:53:05,814 --> 00:53:09,785
It happened exactly a week
ago at about the same time.

662
00:53:10,218 --> 00:53:14,190
- Exactly $1,500 again.
- Bless me!

663
00:53:14,257 --> 00:53:18,262
All you gotta do is sit in that old
rocker every Thursday at 3:30 and say:

664
00:53:18,327 --> 00:53:21,604
"Rosalinda needs
shoes," and collect $1,500.

665
00:53:22,365 --> 00:53:25,005
It's funny how
simple life can be.

666
00:53:51,029 --> 00:53:52,372
It's coming down.

667
00:54:31,405 --> 00:54:35,114
- Hello, Petrushka.
- Here's your pies and cake, Mr. Amendola.

668
00:54:35,209 --> 00:54:37,519
- Thanks.
- And here's a check.

669
00:54:37,578 --> 00:54:40,354
Your share of our
profits for this month.

670
00:54:41,949 --> 00:54:44,555
You cast your dough upon
the waters, it circulates...

671
00:54:44,618 --> 00:54:47,122
and it comes back
strawberry shortcake.

672
00:54:47,222 --> 00:54:49,725
- Thanks, partner.
- Thank you, Mr. Amendola.

673
00:54:54,129 --> 00:54:57,508
- Who was it, Papa?
- Petrushka, our baker.

674
00:55:04,407 --> 00:55:05,681
Oh, mama.

675
00:55:08,945 --> 00:55:09,946
<i>Look.</i>

676
00:55:10,780 --> 00:55:13,386
<i>This is our share
of the month's profit.</i>

677
00:55:14,751 --> 00:55:18,393
<i>How much did we decide to contribute
to buy shoes for European children?</i>

678
00:55:18,455 --> 00:55:19,763
$600.Why?

679
00:55:19,823 --> 00:55:23,168
Deposit this, and we
can make it $1,000.

680
00:55:23,527 --> 00:55:25,507
That's fine.

681
00:55:26,497 --> 00:55:31,207
It's all over town. This Amendola
character gave $1,025 for kids' shoes.

682
00:55:31,268 --> 00:55:33,408
He lives in a stable
and sleeps on straw.

683
00:55:33,471 --> 00:55:36,384
He doesn't work. It don't add
up. Where does he get the money?

684
00:55:36,741 --> 00:55:40,484
He gave $2,500 for
European children.

685
00:55:40,945 --> 00:55:43,482
- What line of business is he in?
- Nobody knows.

686
00:55:43,815 --> 00:55:47,696
Read all about it! Local man
gives thousands to charity!

687
00:55:47,753 --> 00:55:49,426
Read all about it!

688
00:55:50,022 --> 00:55:53,231
When they moved in, they
didn't have a nickel to their name.

689
00:55:53,292 --> 00:55:56,762
Two months later, he buys a
shipload of shoes for foreign kids.

690
00:55:57,263 --> 00:55:59,072
Now, how do you figure that?

691
00:55:59,465 --> 00:56:04,040
I'd like a Super-Duper
Triple-Decker Banana Delight.

692
00:56:04,237 --> 00:56:05,341
Coming UP-

693
00:56:05,439 --> 00:56:06,782
You know what I think?

694
00:56:06,840 --> 00:56:10,583
He's printing his own money, right
there in that shack of yours, Mr. Dingle.

695
00:56:10,644 --> 00:56:14,251
I hate gossip. But I just
heard from a friend of mine...

696
00:56:14,314 --> 00:56:17,022
whose wife told him,
and she swears it's true...

697
00:56:17,417 --> 00:56:20,194
that Amendola is a
big gangster in hiding.

698
00:56:20,922 --> 00:56:24,495
And if I was you, Mr. Dingle,
I'd notify the police right away.

699
00:56:24,759 --> 00:56:27,603
Notify the FBI. I
saw it in a movie.{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Federal_Bureau_of_Investigation}

700
00:56:27,728 --> 00:56:29,799
<i>Someone should write
them an anonymous letter.</i>

701
00:56:29,864 --> 00:56:33,641
Sure, write anonymous letters.
Report him to the police. Put him in jail.

702
00:56:33,701 --> 00:56:36,546
The guy has to be punished
because he has a big heart.

703
00:56:36,605 --> 00:56:40,417
Pipe down, Pete. I told you
never to argue with the customers.

704
00:56:41,176 --> 00:56:42,177
Sorry.

705
00:56:42,678 --> 00:56:45,682
I think it's all a lot of hooey.
You know what I heard?

706
00:56:45,747 --> 00:56:49,160
<i>A man comes to see the pretty
Amendola girl every day in a big car.</i>

707
00:56:49,218 --> 00:56:50,960
- We can't hear you.
- Louder.

708
00:56:51,020 --> 00:56:54,331
A man comes to see their
daughter every day in a big car.

709
00:56:54,390 --> 00:56:56,870
True. He's a Broadway
agent. What about him?

710
00:56:56,926 --> 00:56:59,270
- Like a little strawberry?
- Sure.

711
00:57:00,497 --> 00:57:04,445
<i>He's the individual that floods Amendola
with money, on account of that girl.</i>

712
00:57:04,501 --> 00:57:06,448
- Some whipped cream?
- Love that.

713
00:57:06,637 --> 00:57:08,639
That's what I say about actors.

714
00:57:09,106 --> 00:57:12,553
People like the Amendolas can
contaminate a whole community.

715
00:57:12,943 --> 00:57:15,287
<i>Just like one rotten
apple in a barrel.</i>

716
00:57:19,450 --> 00:57:20,485
Pete!

717
00:57:20,551 --> 00:57:22,156
Oh, my goodness!

718
00:57:24,022 --> 00:57:25,797
I forgot the cherry.

719
00:57:34,466 --> 00:57:36,674
- What will it be?
- Bourbon and water.

720
00:57:37,637 --> 00:57:40,083
- Hi, Pete.
- Hi.

721
00:57:40,139 --> 00:57:42,312
Say, I had dinner
at the drugstore...

722
00:57:42,508 --> 00:57:46,422
and I heard you pushed a chocolate sundae
into a perfectly good customer's kisser.

723
00:57:46,479 --> 00:57:49,323
- Is it true?
- No, it was a Banana Delight.

724
00:57:50,516 --> 00:57:52,827
You're hurt, Pete.
Who hurt you, boy?

725
00:57:52,886 --> 00:57:56,026
Nobody. I just
counted my money...

726
00:57:56,156 --> 00:57:59,399
and I discovered I was 16
cents short of my first million.

727
00:57:59,626 --> 00:58:01,037
<i>That makes me sad.</i>

728
00:58:03,096 --> 00:58:05,770
Listen, Pete, if you're
open to a proposition...

729
00:58:05,832 --> 00:58:07,938
I know how to make
some real dough.

730
00:58:08,002 --> 00:58:09,982
<i>Get this. A friend of mine...</i>

731
00:58:11,873 --> 00:58:16,015
Look, Eddie, why don't you make
like a ballerina and dance away?

732
00:58:16,311 --> 00:58:19,451
<i>Here, stick this in the jukebox
and play something loud.</i>

733
00:58:21,249 --> 00:58:23,924
This friend of mine is
drilling for oil in California.

734
00:58:23,986 --> 00:58:27,433
He's down 9,000 feet.
Got 500 more to go.

735
00:58:27,790 --> 00:58:31,465
So now it's oil stocks. Don't
you ever give up, Mulligan?

736
00:58:31,594 --> 00:58:35,633
But, Pete, for only $2,000, you can
grab yourself 20 percent interest...

737
00:58:35,698 --> 00:58:37,678
and become a
millionaire in no time.

738
00:58:37,733 --> 00:58:41,341
Are you out of your mind?
Where would I get $2,000?

739
00:58:41,605 --> 00:58:43,209
How about asking your old man?

740
00:58:43,273 --> 00:58:46,379
Don't be ridiculous. My
father gets a small pension.

741
00:58:46,710 --> 00:58:50,681
- He's happy if he can make ends meet.
- That's what you think, Junior.

742
00:58:51,548 --> 00:58:54,587
My brother-in-law, who happens
to be a policeman at the bank...

743
00:58:54,652 --> 00:58:58,964
says that old man of yours has been
cashing pretty large checks for weeks now.

744
00:58:59,223 --> 00:59:02,670
Wait a minute. I did see him
coming out of the bank once.

745
00:59:03,361 --> 00:59:06,137
- He gets a letter from them every week.
- You see?

746
00:59:06,197 --> 00:59:08,643
All you have to do is
ask him to back you.

747
00:59:08,699 --> 00:59:11,203
<i>What father could
refuse such a proposition?</i>

748
00:59:11,270 --> 00:59:14,547
Tell me, Mulligan, is
this really on the level?

749
00:59:14,606 --> 00:59:16,085
Tell you what I'll do.

750
00:59:16,141 --> 00:59:19,350
I'll introduce you to my friend.
He'll show you the papers.

751
00:59:19,411 --> 00:59:22,290
And besides, he'll give
you all the security you want.

752
00:59:22,347 --> 00:59:23,951
I'll see you tomorrow.

753
00:59:26,352 --> 00:59:30,027
There must be a screw loose in your
head. Who's got $2,000? What $2,000?

754
00:59:30,089 --> 00:59:33,434
I know you have it. You've been
seen in the bank cashing large checks.

755
00:59:33,493 --> 00:59:36,269
That's a lie. Now get out. I
don't want to hear any more.

756
00:59:36,329 --> 00:59:39,208
I'll pay you back. I'll
repay you 10 times over.

757
00:59:39,265 --> 00:59:41,576
But I haven't
got it, I tell you!

758
00:59:42,136 --> 00:59:44,480
Pa, you must give it
to me. You simply must.

759
00:59:44,538 --> 00:59:48,486
Get out, you crazy fool. Get out before I
break that blasted tuba over your head.

760
00:59:48,542 --> 00:59:49,782
Get out!

761
01:00:12,468 --> 01:00:14,470
- Goodbye.
- I'll walk you there.

762
01:00:14,537 --> 01:00:16,016
No, that's all right.

763
01:00:16,072 --> 01:00:18,211
- Sure you can get in all right?
- Sure.

764
01:00:18,274 --> 01:00:19,378
Good night.

765
01:00:25,514 --> 01:00:26,515
Oh, Pete.

766
01:00:27,583 --> 01:00:29,222
Hello, Rosalinda.

767
01:00:29,786 --> 01:00:32,198
It's a beautiful
night, isn't it?

768
01:00:33,990 --> 01:00:36,095
- Mind if I join you?
- No.

769
01:00:36,793 --> 01:00:38,101
Not at all.

770
01:00:41,665 --> 01:00:43,167
I wanna talk to you.

771
01:00:43,768 --> 01:00:45,145
What is it, Pete?

772
01:00:46,871 --> 01:00:49,909
That is, I have no
right to say this, but...

773
01:00:50,174 --> 01:00:54,520
Say it anyhow, Pete. It might
be something I'd like to hear.

774
01:00:56,047 --> 01:00:59,825
It's the money your father's spending.
They think it's coming from Davis.

775
01:00:59,885 --> 01:01:01,262
- From Phil?
- Yeah.

776
01:01:02,187 --> 01:01:04,167
<i>They say he's in love with you.</i>

777
01:01:04,223 --> 01:01:06,464
You don't believe
that, do you, Pete?

778
01:01:06,525 --> 01:01:09,529
I can see how anyone might
get a wrong impression...

779
01:01:09,595 --> 01:01:11,541
about Phil and me, I mean.

780
01:01:11,597 --> 01:01:14,077
<i>But there isn't anything
between us,</i> Pete.

781
01:01:14,133 --> 01:01:17,775
I can't explain about the money,
because there isn't any explanation, but...

782
01:01:17,837 --> 01:01:19,680
There's no need for one...

783
01:01:20,707 --> 01:01:23,688
not about Davis, or the money...

784
01:01:24,911 --> 01:01:26,083
or anything.

785
01:01:26,880 --> 01:01:28,484
Then, what is it, Pete?

786
01:01:29,382 --> 01:01:32,956
Why are we sitting here like
this, when all you have to say is...

787
01:01:49,304 --> 01:01:51,250
What's the use, Rosalinda?

788
01:01:52,474 --> 01:01:54,784
<i>All right, suppose I
am in love with you.</i>

789
01:01:55,043 --> 01:01:56,681
That's not enough.

790
01:01:57,446 --> 01:02:00,689
$2,000, plus more luck
than I dare hope for...

791
01:02:01,416 --> 01:02:03,556
and we might be able
to do something about it.

792
01:02:03,619 --> 01:02:05,826
A marriage license
only costs $2.

793
01:02:06,489 --> 01:02:08,332
Yeah, but how could we live?

794
01:02:09,091 --> 01:02:11,230
- We could manage.
- How?

795
01:02:11,961 --> 01:02:16,205
By putting windows in my tuba
and converting it into a Quonset hut?

796
01:02:17,033 --> 01:02:20,447
My mother and father didn't have
a penny when they were married.

797
01:02:20,504 --> 01:02:23,917
<i>And Papa says that was the
happiest time in their whole lives.</i>

798
01:02:25,609 --> 01:02:26,713
Very true.

799
01:02:28,545 --> 01:02:31,583
That's great. But I
can't even juggle.

800
01:02:32,516 --> 01:02:36,021
We'll have to take that up
the first thing in the morning.

801
01:02:37,388 --> 01:02:39,732
But why must we have $2,000?

802
01:02:39,824 --> 01:02:43,135
It's an oil deal, and
it might come in.

803
01:02:43,194 --> 01:02:46,073
Then again, it might
not. But if it did...

804
01:02:47,999 --> 01:02:50,913
What's the use trying to
dream my way out of it?

805
01:02:52,304 --> 01:02:55,148
Pete, maybe Papa
can lend you the money.

806
01:02:56,275 --> 01:02:58,619
- Maybe.
- But $2,000?

807
01:02:59,311 --> 01:03:02,588
- You can ask him.
- I'm sorry, Rosalinda.

808
01:03:03,015 --> 01:03:07,055
I'm superstitious about borrowing
money that comes from heaven.

809
01:03:53,803 --> 01:03:55,180
Oh, Pete.

810
01:03:55,738 --> 01:03:57,240
Mr. Amendola.

811
01:03:59,042 --> 01:04:02,512
I couldn't help overhearing you
and Rosalinda talking out here.

812
01:04:02,779 --> 01:04:05,953
It's not that I was eavesdrooping,
or that I meant to listen.

813
01:04:06,015 --> 01:04:08,826
I just had to get up and
fill the cold water bottle.

814
01:04:08,886 --> 01:04:11,230
You see, my feet
get very hot at night.

815
01:04:11,288 --> 01:04:13,199
I hope we didn't disturb you.

816
01:04:13,591 --> 01:04:17,903
What disturbs me is this idea you
got about Rosalinda and this guy Davis.

817
01:04:18,295 --> 01:04:20,400
Believe me,
there's nothing to it.

818
01:04:20,931 --> 01:04:23,708
Why, if you'd look with your
heart instead of your eyes...

819
01:04:23,768 --> 01:04:26,339
you could see where
her best intentions are.

820
01:04:26,538 --> 01:04:30,213
Look, Mr. Amendola, it
isn't Rosalinda. It's me.

821
01:04:30,275 --> 01:04:34,485
Sure it's you. I heard what
you says about being broke.

822
01:04:34,579 --> 01:04:38,858
And if you ask me, I think your
get-up-and-go has got up and went.

823
01:04:39,318 --> 01:04:41,161
What you need is confidence.

824
01:04:41,754 --> 01:04:46,225
And listen. About that
$2,000, I'd be very glad to...

825
01:04:46,292 --> 01:04:48,772
I couldn't take your
money, Mr. Amendola.

826
01:04:49,161 --> 01:04:51,300
Of course, the oil
might come in...

827
01:04:51,497 --> 01:04:54,479
but right now, it's just a hole
in the ground in California.

828
01:04:54,534 --> 01:04:58,846
So what? Uncle Sam put
money in a hole in the ground...

829
01:04:58,905 --> 01:05:01,317
and it turned out
to be Fort Knox.

830
01:05:01,375 --> 01:05:03,480
And I can take a risk, too.

831
01:05:03,543 --> 01:05:06,114
<i>But let's talk about
it in the morning.</i>

832
01:05:13,021 --> 01:05:16,969
- Mama, where's Rosalinda?
- Went for a walk with the boy next door.

833
01:05:17,525 --> 01:05:21,132
You know, Papa, I think
that's getting serious.

834
01:05:21,195 --> 01:05:24,871
Serious? They might get
married and have a couple of kids.

835
01:05:25,134 --> 01:05:28,081
- But that's as far as it would go.
- Oh, Papa.

836
01:05:33,275 --> 01:05:35,949
Mama, remember this?

837
01:05:37,212 --> 01:05:38,213
Vaguely-

838
01:05:38,781 --> 01:05:42,889
Some boy who was courting me
used to sing it to me 22 years ago.

839
01:05:43,453 --> 01:05:45,330
Who was that, now?

840
01:05:45,655 --> 01:05:49,831
Was it the wealthy cattleman from
Montana, or that drummer from Boston?

841
01:05:50,226 --> 01:05:52,331
Mama, that was me.

842
01:05:53,329 --> 01:05:57,779
Of course it was, Papa. And I've been
singing myself to sleep with it ever since.

843
01:05:58,335 --> 01:05:59,609
<i>Sing it, Papa.</i>

844
01:06:17,589 --> 01:06:18,897
I remember.

845
01:07:12,914 --> 01:07:15,088
- Yes, gentlemen?
- We're not together.

846
01:07:15,151 --> 01:07:19,258
I'm Lieutenant Saunders, Police. I'm
looking for a man named Louis Amendola.

847
01:07:19,655 --> 01:07:20,690
That's me.

848
01:07:20,756 --> 01:07:23,293
I'm Inspector Tainey,
Bureau of Internal Revenue.

849
01:07:23,359 --> 01:07:25,168
- May I come in?
- Sure.

850
01:07:31,568 --> 01:07:34,344
- What did you do, Papa?
- I don't know.

851
01:07:34,571 --> 01:07:37,609
Unless I've been burning
the incinerator after-hours.

852
01:07:38,041 --> 01:07:40,385
Gentlemen, this is my wife.

853
01:07:43,280 --> 01:07:45,488
Now all we need is the FBI.

854
01:07:46,984 --> 01:07:48,224
<i>Pardon me.</i>

855
01:07:51,022 --> 01:07:53,662
Callahan, FBI. Are
you Mr. Amendola?

856
01:07:53,891 --> 01:07:56,201
- Yes, sir. Won't you come in?
- Thanks.

857
01:08:00,131 --> 01:08:02,339
Me and my big, fat mouth.

858
01:08:09,842 --> 01:08:12,322
Mr. Amendola, I'd like to
ask you a few questions...

859
01:08:12,377 --> 01:08:14,618
Do you mind? I was here first.

860
01:08:15,113 --> 01:08:17,822
Mr. Amendola, the Department
of Internal Revenue...

861
01:08:17,884 --> 01:08:20,956
would like some information
regarding your source of income.

862
01:08:21,020 --> 01:08:22,624
- Income?
- A report...

863
01:08:22,689 --> 01:08:24,191
Please. May I?

864
01:08:24,624 --> 01:08:28,197
We have a report showing you've
been spending large sums of money...

865
01:08:28,261 --> 01:08:31,869
the source of which is not indicated
in your previous income-tax reports.

866
01:08:31,932 --> 01:08:36,108
We're a little curious, Mr. Amendola, to
know where the money is coming from.

867
01:08:36,270 --> 01:08:38,841
We saved a lot when we
were the Human Pyramid.

868
01:08:38,906 --> 01:08:41,580
And I took that money
and I invested it wisely.

869
01:08:41,876 --> 01:08:46,018
I'll have you know that I've majored
in Economics at Cranfus College.

870
01:08:46,080 --> 01:08:48,254
Cranfus? Never
heard of that college.

871
01:08:48,316 --> 01:08:51,160
That's like saying you
never heard of Joe Tupis.

872
01:08:51,219 --> 01:08:53,756
- Who's he?
- He's the President of Cranfus!

873
01:08:53,822 --> 01:08:56,894
Mr. Amendola, we're not
interested in your education.

874
01:08:56,992 --> 01:08:59,905
We just want to know where
you're getting the money.

875
01:09:01,229 --> 01:09:04,643
All right, gentlemen.
I'll tell you the truth.

876
01:09:05,735 --> 01:09:08,978
I'm the long lost son of
a very wealthy typhoon.

877
01:09:11,274 --> 01:09:15,518
When I was only two days old, a nurse
turned her back while changing me...

878
01:09:15,645 --> 01:09:19,116
and I was stolen from the cradle
by a band of starving gypsies.

879
01:09:20,450 --> 01:09:25,365
A few months ago, the king of the gypsies
came and told me who my father was.

880
01:09:38,336 --> 01:09:40,145
<i>I see you don't believe me.</i>

881
01:09:40,939 --> 01:09:43,442
All right, I'll tell
you the truth.

882
01:09:49,982 --> 01:09:52,826
I was down at the beach,
digging with my pail and shovel...

883
01:09:52,885 --> 01:09:56,094
when suddenly my shovel struck
the top of an ironbound chest.

884
01:09:56,155 --> 01:09:58,567
- Pirates' treasure?
- That's right.

885
01:09:58,791 --> 01:10:02,967
That does it. Look here, Amendola,
you've been reported for everything...

886
01:10:03,028 --> 01:10:06,169
from swiping tires off baby
carriages to operating your own mint.

887
01:10:06,232 --> 01:10:08,803
- What's your racket?
- Leave him alone.

888
01:10:08,868 --> 01:10:13,078
Why can't we tell them the
truth, darling? It's so simple.

889
01:10:13,139 --> 01:10:16,951
- All right, lady, what is the truth?
- Where does the money come from?

890
01:10:18,511 --> 01:10:20,320
From heaven.

891
01:10:24,919 --> 01:10:26,921
That explains everything.

892
01:10:27,355 --> 01:10:30,529
But it's the truth. It comes
straight from heaven.

893
01:10:30,591 --> 01:10:34,505
- $1,500 every week.
- On the dot.

894
01:10:35,663 --> 01:10:38,611
I like the one about
the pirates' chest better.

895
01:10:39,368 --> 01:10:42,611
<i>Come on, Amendola, let's go
and tell it to the boys at the station.</i>

896
01:10:42,671 --> 01:10:45,015
But I can prove it. I
can prove every word...

897
01:10:45,073 --> 01:10:48,282
- Now, how can you prove it?
- It's easy.

898
01:10:48,410 --> 01:10:51,516
I put this old rocking chair
in the middle of the room...

899
01:10:51,580 --> 01:10:53,720
sit down there, and
say a little prayer.

900
01:10:53,783 --> 01:10:56,957
And when I say,
"Rosalinda needs shoes..."

901
01:10:57,020 --> 01:10:59,091
The money comes down like rain.

902
01:10:59,155 --> 01:11:02,796
- All right, Mrs. Amendola, show us.
- Pull up the chair and say the prayer.

903
01:11:02,859 --> 01:11:05,271
Wait a minute. She
can't do it today.

904
01:11:05,361 --> 01:11:08,673
The miracle happens on
Thursday between 3:00 and 3:30.

905
01:11:10,601 --> 01:11:15,175
- We'll be here Thursday at 3:00.
- Thursday at 3:00.

906
01:11:28,119 --> 01:11:30,395
I should doubt my veracity.

907
01:11:42,701 --> 01:11:43,805
Look.

908
01:11:53,412 --> 01:11:56,257
And I hear they're both
doing tremendous business.

909
01:11:56,516 --> 01:11:59,224
They are? All right,
come on, let's go.

910
01:12:02,489 --> 01:12:03,593
Look.

911
01:12:14,869 --> 01:12:16,439
How's business, Mr. Molinari?

912
01:12:16,504 --> 01:12:19,280
Couldn't be better. More
orders than we can take care of.

913
01:12:19,340 --> 01:12:20,785
Come on, Katie, let's go.

914
01:12:20,842 --> 01:12:23,789
Clever man, that Amendola.
Soon he'll own the whole town.

915
01:12:23,845 --> 01:12:28,022
You could've done the same, if you'd
invested your money instead of hiding it.

916
01:12:29,118 --> 01:12:30,620
<i>Marx and Amendola Garage.</i>

917
01:12:31,220 --> 01:12:33,393
- Look at that.
- No, I won't look!

918
01:12:33,455 --> 01:12:36,129
And don't stop every
second. I'm getting tired of this.

919
01:12:36,191 --> 01:12:40,162
You know what I'm gonna do? Lam
gonna raise Amendola's rent again.

920
01:12:40,329 --> 01:12:41,866
As of today! Come on.

921
01:12:44,134 --> 01:12:48,014
Due to the increased cost of living,
I must raise the rent of this place.

922
01:12:48,071 --> 01:12:49,072
Again?

923
01:12:49,472 --> 01:12:53,079
This is the eighth time you've
raised the rent since we moved in.

924
01:12:53,143 --> 01:12:55,953
You're forcing me to join
Landlords Anonymous.

925
01:12:56,447 --> 01:12:58,484
You can move out if you want to.

926
01:12:58,649 --> 01:13:00,993
Let's not be too
hasty, Mr. Dingle.

927
01:13:01,152 --> 01:13:04,531
You might be right. Maybe
the cost of living has increased.

928
01:13:04,889 --> 01:13:07,870
I wouldn't know, I haven't
done today's shopping yet.

929
01:13:08,726 --> 01:13:10,467
How much more do you want?

930
01:13:12,731 --> 01:13:16,736
- $30.
- $30? $125 a month? Why, it's robbery!

931
01:13:16,868 --> 01:13:19,246
If you think that, you can
start packing right now.

932
01:13:19,304 --> 01:13:21,716
He didn't mean that,
Mr. Dingle. Did you, Papa?

933
01:13:21,773 --> 01:13:23,047
Of course not.

934
01:13:23,542 --> 01:13:25,385
I apologize, Mr. Dingle.

935
01:13:25,644 --> 01:13:29,286
Why, for only $125 a month,
where else could you find a place...

936
01:13:29,348 --> 01:13:32,386
where the sunshine comes
streaming through the keyhole all day?

937
01:13:32,451 --> 01:13:34,556
You're right. You can't
take advantage of me...

938
01:13:34,620 --> 01:13:38,033
- like those bankrupt shop owners.
- Now, look here.

939
01:13:38,090 --> 01:13:40,661
<i>I only helped them out
because no one else would.</i>

940
01:13:40,726 --> 01:13:44,106
<i>And besides, I'm not making
money on all those investments.</i>

941
01:13:44,298 --> 01:13:46,574
<i>For instance, your
son's oil venture.</i>

942
01:13:46,733 --> 01:13:49,509
<i>I'll surely lose the
$2,000 I invested in that.</i>

943
01:13:50,003 --> 01:13:52,882
You deserve to. He
couldn't take me for a sucker.

944
01:13:52,973 --> 01:13:55,749
- I wouldn't give him a nickel.
- If you did...

945
01:13:55,809 --> 01:13:58,723
you'd probably shortchange
him, you tight old buzzard.

946
01:13:58,780 --> 01:14:02,626
That's done it. Another word out of
you, and I'll punch you in the nose!

947
01:14:02,684 --> 01:14:06,257
- Why, I'll...
- Papa! Mr. Dingle!

948
01:14:06,321 --> 01:14:10,235
Why, you two should be friends,
if only for our children's sake.

949
01:14:10,291 --> 01:14:12,168
They love one another.

950
01:14:12,360 --> 01:14:15,001
Who knows? They might
get married someday.

951
01:14:15,064 --> 01:14:19,809
No son of mine will ever marry a
girl with your daughter's reputation.

952
01:14:19,902 --> 01:14:21,609
Get out!

953
01:14:24,173 --> 01:14:25,948
Papa, the chair.

954
01:14:26,175 --> 01:14:28,621
- It's broken.
- Get me some nails and glue.

955
01:14:28,677 --> 01:14:31,386
I'll fix it. Hurry! It's
almost time to pray.

956
01:14:54,705 --> 01:14:58,209
"We are sorry to
inform you that..."

957
01:14:59,677 --> 01:15:00,883
Katie!

958
01:15:03,649 --> 01:15:04,787
Yes, Frank?

959
01:15:06,652 --> 01:15:08,757
Our gold mine is exhausted.

960
01:15:09,321 --> 01:15:11,494
There'll be no more checks.

961
01:15:16,829 --> 01:15:20,367
- Let's get going. It's almost 3:30.
- Okay.

962
01:15:24,537 --> 01:15:26,175
<i>All right, Mama.</i>

963
01:15:28,608 --> 01:15:29,814
Say it.

964
01:15:31,811 --> 01:15:33,723
Rosalinda needs shoes.

965
01:15:40,187 --> 01:15:43,134
- Look...
- Gentlemen, a little patience.

966
01:15:43,390 --> 01:15:47,304
<i>$1, 500 is</i> a <i>lot of money.
Sometimes we gotta ask twice.</i>

967
01:15:48,863 --> 01:15:50,467
Try it again, Mama.

968
01:15:53,768 --> 01:15:55,679
Rosalinda needs shoes.

969
01:15:58,873 --> 01:16:00,284
Once again.

970
01:16:02,577 --> 01:16:06,526
Rosalinda needs shoes.

971
01:16:11,787 --> 01:16:13,596
I guess you're right, Mama.

972
01:16:14,657 --> 01:16:16,568
<i>It seems the miracle is over.</i>

973
01:16:21,865 --> 01:16:25,745
I threw away $1,500 a
week when I threw this chair.

974
01:16:26,536 --> 01:16:27,981
It's not the chair.

975
01:16:28,171 --> 01:16:31,550
It's that we have no
excuse to ask for any more.

976
01:16:32,175 --> 01:16:35,680
You're right. But we're
doing all right, now.

977
01:16:36,147 --> 01:16:38,627
<i>There's a lot of poor
people praying in this world.</i>

978
01:16:38,683 --> 01:16:40,788
<i>Now ifs their tum for miracles.</i>

979
01:17:02,074 --> 01:17:04,748
I'm moving out of here the
first thing tomorrow morning.

980
01:17:04,810 --> 01:17:07,257
Now even my cigarettes
don't come down.

981
01:17:07,313 --> 01:17:10,317
I'm afraid you won't
have to wait till then.

982
01:17:10,383 --> 01:17:12,590
You'll have to move
now, down to the station.

983
01:17:12,652 --> 01:17:15,690
Not so fast, Lieutenant. This is
a tax matter. He goes with me.

984
01:17:15,755 --> 01:17:19,066
Wait a minute, you two. I don't
know what kind of a matter this is...

985
01:17:19,125 --> 01:17:21,071
but he's going with
me for questioning.

986
01:17:21,127 --> 01:17:24,541
- Question him at the station.
- I don't want to.

987
01:17:24,632 --> 01:17:26,475
I want to question
him at my field office.

988
01:17:26,534 --> 01:17:29,310
I don't care where you question
him. He's going with me now.

989
01:17:29,370 --> 01:17:31,441
Over my broken
and splintered body.

990
01:17:31,505 --> 01:17:35,453
I have no objection to taking him
over your broken and splintered body.

991
01:17:35,509 --> 01:17:38,980
Gentlemen, let's have
cooperation. Let him go with me.

992
01:17:40,081 --> 01:17:41,458
Good night, Pete.

993
01:17:42,984 --> 01:17:44,725
Good night, Rosalinda.

994
01:17:48,256 --> 01:17:50,532
Mama, Papa, what's the matter?

995
01:17:50,592 --> 01:17:54,700
These people don't understand
where we're getting our money from.

996
01:17:54,764 --> 01:17:58,405
So I'm going downtown and
explaining it. You stay here with Mother.

997
01:17:58,768 --> 01:18:00,577
Is there anything I can do?

998
01:18:02,071 --> 01:18:03,982
Pardon us a moment, will you?

999
01:18:05,408 --> 01:18:08,288
Look, I don't know how
long I'm gonna be gone.

1000
01:18:08,846 --> 01:18:11,554
Kinda keep an eye on
things till I get back, will you?

1001
01:18:11,682 --> 01:18:14,788
Sure. But there's
trouble. Is it serious?

1002
01:18:14,885 --> 01:18:16,523
There's nothing to it.

1003
01:18:16,587 --> 01:18:20,000
With time off for good
behavior, I may only get life.

1004
01:18:21,291 --> 01:18:24,273
- All right, gentlemen, whose car do I go in?
- Mine.

1005
01:18:27,599 --> 01:18:29,044
I smell smoke.

1006
01:18:29,901 --> 01:18:30,902
So do I.

1007
01:18:35,106 --> 01:18:39,317
But, Dad, Mr. Amendola's in serious
trouble. Maybe the $2,000 would help him.

1008
01:18:39,545 --> 01:18:42,048
- You've got to give it to him.
- I think you should.

1009
01:18:42,114 --> 01:18:44,390
- He helped Pete.
- Helped him how?

1010
01:18:44,450 --> 01:18:47,158
To make an idiot out of
himself by giving him $2,000".

1011
01:18:47,219 --> 01:18:49,927
For that crook
Mulligan. I won't do it!

1012
01:18:49,989 --> 01:18:52,094
I won't give Amendola one penny.

1013
01:18:52,157 --> 01:18:54,637
Let him fry in his own
fat. He belongs in jail.

1014
01:18:54,693 --> 01:18:56,503
But, Dad, this is important.

1015
01:18:56,696 --> 01:18:59,176
Frank, you're a hard-hearted,
money-hoarding...

1016
01:18:59,266 --> 01:19:03,078
I'm going upstairs, where I don't
have to listen to either one of you.

1017
01:19:13,047 --> 01:19:16,290
Help! I'm choking!

1018
01:19:20,488 --> 01:19:21,762
Don't worry, Dad.

1019
01:19:21,822 --> 01:19:23,028
<i>Come on, Dad.</i>

1020
01:19:23,891 --> 01:19:26,202
- I gotta get my money!
- Forget your money.

1021
01:19:26,261 --> 01:19:28,207
It's in a hole behind the bed.

1022
01:19:30,198 --> 01:19:31,905
Mom, I've got him.

1023
01:19:32,701 --> 01:19:35,238
The money! I want
to get the money!

1024
01:19:35,470 --> 01:19:36,744
<i>Let me go!</i>

1025
01:19:53,022 --> 01:19:57,597
The polar bear rug with the radio!
I'll miss The <i>Jimmy</i> Durante Show.

1026
01:19:57,661 --> 01:19:58,833
Here it is, Papa!

1027
01:20:40,506 --> 01:20:44,478
- Thousands of dollars. It's all gone.
- Heaven knows you deserve it.

1028
01:20:44,544 --> 01:20:45,852
Are we insured, Pa?

1029
01:20:45,946 --> 01:20:48,984
Heck, no. Those chiselers
don't get any of my money, no sir.

1030
01:20:50,017 --> 01:20:51,928
Good heavens, I'm not insured!

1031
01:20:53,754 --> 01:20:57,793
You have a very smart father. He
trusts nobody but the hole behind the bed.

1032
01:20:59,259 --> 01:21:01,331
Pete, I'm so glad
you're all right.

1033
01:21:01,396 --> 01:21:06,209
Thousands of dollars burning right
now. Brand-new, crisp $100 bills.

1034
01:21:06,267 --> 01:21:08,372
He put them in a
hole behind his bed.

1035
01:21:08,436 --> 01:21:12,543
Every Thursday,
$1,500. Now it's all gone.

1036
01:21:20,382 --> 01:21:22,157
"Rosalinda needs shoes"?

1037
01:21:26,722 --> 01:21:28,793
Pull yourself
together, Mr. Dingle.

1038
01:21:28,857 --> 01:21:31,737
<i>HI rebuild the house.
It'll be better than new.</i>

1039
01:21:32,228 --> 01:21:35,334
- You will?
- I don't know what to say.

1040
01:21:35,398 --> 01:21:37,674
It's perfectly all
right, Mrs. Dingle.

1041
01:21:37,901 --> 01:21:40,313
<i>Why, anything to
help a good neighbor.</i>

1042
01:21:43,573 --> 01:21:47,044
The way I see it, now this is
very definitely only a tax matter.

1043
01:21:47,111 --> 01:21:49,717
- This makes things much simpler.
- What do you mean?

1044
01:21:49,780 --> 01:21:52,283
Who owes the tax?
Amendola, or this other guy?

1045
01:21:52,349 --> 01:21:53,953
Naturally, the...

1046
01:21:55,152 --> 01:21:58,656
I guess Amendola. I don't
know. They both had incomes.

1047
01:21:58,756 --> 01:22:02,398
- Maybe they both owe us.
- How? It was this other fellow's income.

1048
01:22:02,461 --> 01:22:05,999
Sure. Amendola was only
innocently using the other guy's money.

1049
01:22:06,364 --> 01:22:07,843
It was like a gift.

1050
01:22:07,999 --> 01:22:12,573
In that case, this man not only owes income
tax, he'll have to pay us gift tax, too.

1051
01:22:12,838 --> 01:22:16,285
As far as I'm concerned, the
local law is no longer interested.

1052
01:22:16,341 --> 01:22:18,481
Looks like it's out of
my jurisdiction, too.

1053
01:22:18,544 --> 01:22:22,686
It's too involved for me. I'm
gonna forget all about till March 15.

1054
01:22:24,917 --> 01:22:27,727
- What did you find?
- Nothing but a dead squirrel.

1055
01:22:29,956 --> 01:22:33,871
He isn't dead. He's just overcome by
smoke. All he needs is a little fresh air.

1056
01:22:33,927 --> 01:22:36,237
Wonder what he was
doing in the house.

1057
01:22:42,336 --> 01:22:45,112
<i>That's the place for
a little squirrel to be.</i>

1058
01:22:53,882 --> 01:22:55,919
- Thanks for the lift.
- Okay.

1059
01:23:07,096 --> 01:23:08,097
Rupert.

1060
01:23:10,733 --> 01:23:11,734
<i>Rupert.</i>

1061
01:23:52,411 --> 01:23:54,550
My good old Rupert.

1062
01:23:55,647 --> 01:23:57,718
You haven't changed a bit.

1063
01:23:58,717 --> 01:24:02,164
You smell a little smoky,
though. Now, tell me, how was it?

1064
01:24:02,421 --> 01:24:05,959
How did the other squirrels
treat you? Were they good?

1065
01:24:06,993 --> 01:24:08,472
I've got good news for you.

1066
01:24:08,528 --> 01:24:11,702
I got a job with a circus, and
we start in two weeks in Hoboken.

1067
01:24:11,764 --> 01:24:13,744
Me and two seals.

1068
01:25:07,690 --> 01:25:11,264
- Greetings, Mr. and Mrs. Dingle.
- Mr. Amendola.

1069
01:25:11,328 --> 01:25:13,968
- For your new home, Mrs. Dingle.
- Thank you.

1070
01:25:14,264 --> 01:25:16,505
- Go ahead, Papa.
- Make your speech.

1071
01:25:17,801 --> 01:25:18,802
Mr. Dingle...

1072
01:25:19,303 --> 01:25:21,647
like a caterpillar
has its cocoon...

1073
01:25:21,872 --> 01:25:24,045
like a bird has its nest...

1074
01:25:24,574 --> 01:25:26,885
like the worm has its...

1075
01:25:29,514 --> 01:25:31,494
apple, believe me...

1076
01:25:31,549 --> 01:25:35,258
it's a pleasure to present you
with the key to your new home.

1077
01:25:36,321 --> 01:25:37,664
Mr. Amendola...

1078
01:25:38,122 --> 01:25:40,865
you're the finest, the
noblest, the most generous...

1079
01:25:40,960 --> 01:25:43,065
I wouldn't say that, Mr. Dingle.

1080
01:25:43,128 --> 01:25:44,869
But I'm glad he did.

1081
01:25:45,297 --> 01:25:48,471
I'm overwhelmed. I
can't find words enough...

1082
01:25:48,534 --> 01:25:50,980
Words. What are words?

1083
01:25:51,036 --> 01:25:55,246
If it'll make you any happier, I'll
even carry you across the threshold.

1084
01:25:55,307 --> 01:25:57,310
Thank you, Mr. Amendola.

1085
01:26:11,792 --> 01:26:13,703
Our old home, Frank.

1086
01:26:18,065 --> 01:26:21,410
- And it's nicer than before.
- I'm glad you like it.

1087
01:26:21,802 --> 01:26:26,308
My little wife crocheted the doilies, but
I'll have to take credit for the curtains.

1088
01:26:26,640 --> 01:26:30,418
I would have done a much better
job, only my pinking shears were dull.

1089
01:26:33,281 --> 01:26:34,919
- Pete.
- Mulligan.

1090
01:26:35,350 --> 01:26:38,456
- Why, you dirty, double-crossing...
- No, wait a minute.

1091
01:26:39,254 --> 01:26:40,324
<i>Let me go.</i>

1092
01:26:40,422 --> 01:26:42,903
We're rich, Pete! The
oil is coming like a geyser.

1093
01:26:42,959 --> 01:26:44,700
What? The well came in?

1094
01:26:46,496 --> 01:26:48,407
Rosie, come down here, quick!

1095
01:26:48,498 --> 01:26:49,499
Phil.

1096
01:26:58,041 --> 01:26:59,885
<i>You don't mean it, Phil?</i>

1097
01:26:59,943 --> 01:27:01,889
Hey, look here, Davis!

1098
01:27:02,579 --> 01:27:05,560
Pete, listen. It's your
music. Phil just told me.

1099
01:27:05,616 --> 01:27:08,392
- What is it?
- Be quiet and listen to the radio.

1100
01:27:08,452 --> 01:27:11,729
...The Melody for Two Orphaned
Instruments <i>by</i> Peter Dingle.

1101
01:27:18,196 --> 01:27:20,198
You did this, Rosalinda.

1102
01:27:21,166 --> 01:27:23,407
<i>You see what! Mean
about taking a risk?</i>

1103
01:27:23,468 --> 01:27:27,280
Speaking of risks, Pete, when
are you gonna ask me to marry you?

1104
01:27:27,372 --> 01:27:28,646
Right now.

1105
01:27:29,442 --> 01:27:31,183
They're gonna get married.

1106
01:27:32,745 --> 01:27:34,053
Rosalinda.

1107
01:27:34,513 --> 01:27:35,753
Pete!

1108
01:27:36,882 --> 01:27:41,058
- Amendola, my friend.
- Dingle, old pal.
